 Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs // Restore the saved color scheme preference, or // enact the browser preference if "automatic", // without expecting DOM load to have completed. // // Also restore any saved readingmode and ToC display preferences. function initializeReadingPreferences() { let saved_theme = localStorage.getItem("ar5iv_theme") || "automatic"; if (saved_theme === "automatic") { if (window.matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").matches) { saved_theme = "dark"; } } if (saved_theme == "dark") { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-theme", "dark"); } else { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-theme", "light"); } const tocDisplay = localStorage.getItem('arxiv_html_paper_toc_display'); if (tocDisplay) { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-toc-display", tocDisplay); } const readingMode = localStorage.getItem('arxiv_html_paper_reading_mode'); if (readingMode) { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-reading-mode", readingMode); } } // Run as soon as JS starts, to minimize repainting initializeReadingPreferences(); /* Reading mode hides the chrome and the empty .arxiv-html-header marker: the pinned JS reads the marker's display to know which way to toggle. The marker draws no box. */ .arxiv-html-header { display: contents; } html[data-reading-mode="enabled"] :is(.arxiv-html-site-chrome, .arxiv-html-header, .ds-site-footer) { display: none; } /* The paper controls' icons are blocks, so they sit centred (the pinned JS shows the theme icon by making its span a block). */ .arxiv-html-site-chrome .ds-site-header-tools svg { display: block; width: 18px; height: 18px; flex-shrink: 0; fill: currentColor; } /* ar5iv dims every image in dark mode and puts it on a white wash, for paper figures; arXiv's own render as they are (the logo on the bar, the funder logos on their white chips). Important: ar5iv's dark rules are unlayered, as specific as :root:not([data-theme]) img:not(.ltx_no_dark_filter). */ :is(.ds-site-header, .ds-site-footer) img { filter: none !important; } .ds-site-header img { background-color: transparent !important; } /* Wide (the theme's 1280px): the theme lays the body out as a grid whose full-width top rows were the band's and its sticky reader bar's. The band takes the first and scrolls away; the header, the paper's bar (no site navigation, the paper's controls), takes the second and stays in view, the one sticky bar, with the anchor clearance that requires (DESIGN-POLICIES). The controls show their names. */ @media (min-width: 1280px) { html { scroll-padding-top: 80px; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome { display: contents; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome > #ds-announcement { grid-area: announce; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome > .ds-site-header { grid-area: header; position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 3; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome .tool-icon { display: none; } } /* Narrow: the header scrolls away, and the controls show icons (names kept for screen readers; Why HTML? goes). The theme's contents panel is fixed below where its old sticky bar was, and hidden only once the reader has closed it, so until then it is an invisible box over the header and the paper: it shows only while open, under the chrome, in its place in the page. The paper's footer (build logs, reporting instructions) keeps the text's side inset; the theme gives it none, so it met the screen edges. */ @media (max-width: 1279px) { .arxiv-html-site-chrome .tool-wide { display: none; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome .tool-label { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; overflow: hidden; clip: rect(0 0 0 0); white-space: nowrap; } html:not([data-toc-display="block"]) .ltx_page_navbar { display: none; } .ltx_page_navbar { position: absolute; top: auto; } .arxiv-html-footer { padding-inline: 1rem; } } Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Submit without GitHub Submit in GitHub Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme Abstract 1 Introduction 2 A construction of oriented graphs with large acyclic dichromatic number 3 Acyclic heroes of tournaments 3.1 Proof of Theorem 3.2 Proof of Theorem 3.3 Proof of Theorem 4 Complexity 4.1 Complexity for split digraphs 4.2 A polynomial-time algorithm for Acyclic 2 2 -Dicolouring on tournaments 5 Tight bounds on the acyclic dichromatic number of tournaments 6 Degenerate graphs 7 Remarks and further open problems References License: CC BY-NC-ND 4.0 arXiv:2511.20246v1 [math.CO] 25 Nov 2025 Acyclic dichromatic number of oriented graphs Jørgen Bang-Jensen Affiliation: Department of Mathematics and Computer Science, University of Southern Denmark, Odense, Denmark Affiliation: School of Mathematics, Shandong University, Jinan 250100, China Lucas Picasarri-Arrieta † † thanks: Research supported by JST ASPIRE JPMJAP2302. Affiliation: National Institute of Informatics, Tokyo, Japan Anders Yeo Affiliation: Department of Mathematics and Computer Science, University of Southern Denmark, Odense, Denmark Affiliation: Department of Mathematics and Applied Mathematics, University of Johannesburg, Auckland Park, 2006 South Africa Abstract The dichromatic number χ → ​ ( D ) \vec{\chi}(D) of a digraph D = ( V , A ) D=(V,A) is the minimum number of sets in a partition V 1 , … , V k V_{1},\ldots{},V_{k} of V V into k k subsets so that the induced subdigraph D ⁡ [ V i ] D[V_{i}] is acyclic for each i ∈ [ k ] i\in[k] . This is a generalization of the chromatic number for undirected graphs as a graph has chromatic number at most k k if and only if the complete biorientation ↔ G \hfil\textstyle G\hfil of G G (replace each edge by a directed 2-cycle) has dichromatic number at most k k . In this paper we introduce the acyclic dichromatic number χ → a ​ ( D ) \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}{}(D) of a digraph D D as the minimum number of sets in a partition V 1 , … , V k V_{1},\ldots{},V_{k} of V V into k k subsets so that the induced subdigraph D ⁡ [ V i ] D[V_{i}] is acyclic for each i ∈ [ k ] i\in[k] and each of the bipartite induced subdigraphs D ⁡ [ V i , V j ] D[V_{i},V_{j}] is acyclic for each 1 ⩽ i < j ⩽ k 1\leqslant i<j\leqslant k . This parameter, which resembles the definition of acyclic chromatic number for undirected graphs, has apparently not been studied before. We derive a number of results which display the difference between the dichromatic number and the acyclic dichromatic number, in particular, there are digraphs D D with arbitrarily large χ → a ​ ( D ) − χ → ​ ( D ) \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}{}(D)-\vec{\chi}{}(D) , even among tournaments with dichromatic number 2 and bipartite tournaments (where the dichromatic number is always 2). We prove several complexity results, including that deciding whether χ → a ​ ( D ) ⩽ 2 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}{}(D)\leqslant 2 is NP-complete already for bipartite digraphs, while it is polynomial for tournaments (contrary to the case for dichromatic number). We also generalize the concept of heroes of a tournament to acyclic heroes of tournaments. Keywords: Dichromatic number; acyclic dichromatic number; tournaments; bipartite tournaments; split digraphs; NP-completeness; acyclic heroes. 1 Introduction Terminology not provided below is consistent with [ 7 ] . Given an integer k ∈ ℕ k\in\mathbb{N} , a k k -colouring of a digraph D D is a mapping φ : V ⁡ ( D ) → [ k ] \varphi\colon V(D)\to[k] . The sets V 1 , … , V k V_{1},\dots,V_{k} where V i = φ − 1 ​ ( i ) V_{i}=\varphi^{-1}(i) are called the colour classes of φ \varphi . We say that φ \varphi is a k k -dicolouring if, for every i ∈ [ k ] i\in[k] , D ⁡ [ V i ] D[V_{i}] is acyclic. The dichromatic number of D D is the least integer k ∈ ℕ k\in\mathbb{N} such that D D admits an acyclic k k -dicolouring. The notions of dicolouring and dichromatic number were introduced by Erdős and Neumann-Lara in the late 1970s [ 17 , 26 ] , rediscovered independently by Mohar [ 11 , 25 ] in the 2000s, and gained a lot of attention since then. It turned out that many classical results on graph colouring admit a generalisation to the directed setting involving the dichromatic number, see for instance [ 1 , 2 , 19 , 21 , 22 ] . In this paper, we introduce the notion of acyclic dicolouring of oriented graphs (that is, digraphs with at most one arc between any two vertices). A k k -dicolouring φ \varphi , with colour classes V 1 , … , V k V_{1},\dots,V_{k} , is acyclic if, for every 1 ⩽ i < j ⩽ k 1\leqslant i<j\leqslant k , the bipartite oriented graph D ⁡ [ V i , V j ] D[V_{i},V_{j}] is acyclic. The acyclic dichromatic number of D D , denoted χ → a ​ ( D ) \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D) , is the least integer k ∈ ℕ k\in\mathbb{N} such that D D admits a acyclic k k -dicolouring. Note that χ → a ​ ( D ) \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D) is well-defined, as every oriented graph D D admits a trivial acyclic | V ⁡ ( D ) | |V(D)| -dicolouring. This notion is a directed analogue of the one of acyclic colouring [ 18 ] , which is a colouring of an undirected graph such that any two adjacent vertices receive distinct colours, and the union of any two colour classes induces a forest ( i.e. is acyclic). In particular, if D D is an oriented graph with underlying graph G G , then any acyclic colouring of G G yields an acyclic dicolouring of D D , and in particular χ → a ​ ( D ) ⩽ χ a ​ ( G ) \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant\chi_{\rm a}(G) , where χ a ​ ( G ) \chi_{\rm a}(G) denotes the acyclic chromatic number of G G . Adding the restriction that also the bipartite subdigraphs D ⁡ [ V i , V j ] D[V_{i},V_{j}] are acyclic for each 1 ⩽ i < j ⩽ k 1\leqslant i<j\leqslant k may force us to use (many) more colours in an acyclic dicolouring of a digraph D D than if we just require each D ⁡ [ V i ] D[V_{i}] to be acyclic. This also has the effect that the complexity of deciding whether χ → a ​ ( D ) ⩽ k \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}{(D)}\leqslant k may differ from that of deciding whether χ → ​ ( D ) ⩽ k \vec{\chi}(D)\leqslant k , namely, the first problem may be polynomial for some k k even if the second one is NP-complete. Indeed, as a prominent case, we prove that this is the case for tournaments when k = 2 k=2 . The paper is organized as follows. In Section 2 , we show how to construct (bipartite) digraphs with arbitrarily high acyclic dichromatic number, showing that the difference between the acyclic dichromatic number and the dichromatic number can be arbitrarily high even for bipartite digraphs. We use this to show that the same holds also for the class of tournaments of dichromatic number 2. In Section 3 , we generalize the concept of heroes of tournaments to what we call acyclic heroes for tournaments, that is, tournaments so that every tournament that does not contain one of these as a subtournament has bounded acyclic dichromatic number. We formulate a conjecture describing which tournaments are exactly the acyclic heroes, and provide several results which support this conjecture. In Section 4 , we first use the construction from Section 2 to obtain an easy proof that determining whether χ → a ​ ( D ) ⩽ k \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant k is NP-complete for every k k , even for bipartite digraphs. We also show that the same conclusion holds for split digraphs. We then show that, contrary (assuming P is not equal to NP) to the case for the dichromatic number, one can check in polynomial time whether a tournament has acyclic dichromatic number at most 2. In Section 5 , we derive tight upper bounds for the acyclic dichromatic number of tournaments and in Section 6 we show that every 2-degenerate oriented graph has acyclic dichromatic number at most 2 while there are 3-degenerate oriented graphs with arbitrarily high acyclic dichromatic number. Finally, in Section 7 we pose a number of open problems on acyclic dichromatic numbers of oriented graphs. 2 A construction of oriented graphs with large acyclic dichromatic number It is well-known that the random tournament on n n vertices has dichromatic number Θ ⁡ ( n / log ⁡ n ) \Theta(n/\log n) with high probability, see e.g. [ 9 , Section 4] . In Theorem 24 below, we show that the random tournament on n n vertices has acyclic dichromatic number n − Θ ⁡ ( log ⁡ n ) n-\Theta(\log n) with high probability, so there exist tournaments T T of order n n whose gap between χ → a ​ ( T ) \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}{(T)} and χ → ​ ( T ) \vec{\chi}{(T)} is n ⁡ ( 1 − o ⁡ ( 1 ) ) n(1-o(1)) . In this section, we provide a construction of tournaments with dichromatic number 2 2 but arbitrarily large acyclic dichromatic number that will be used later on. For any undirected graph G = ( V , E ) G=(V,E) , the corresponding symmetric digraph ↔ G \hfil\textstyle G\hfil is obtained by replacing each edge of G G by a directed 2-cycle. We let D ⁡ ( G ) D(G) be the oriented graph obtained from ↔ G \hfil\textstyle G\hfil by performing the operation called vertex splitting in [ 7 , page 134] , that is, each vertex v v is replaced by two vertices v i ​ n , v o ​ u ​ t v_{in},v_{out} and the arc v i ​ n ​ v o ​ u ​ t v_{in}v_{out} and every arc u ​ v uv is replaced by the arc u o ​ u ​ t ​ v i ​ n u_{out}v_{in} . The digraph D ⁡ ( G ) D(G) is bipartite with bipartition ( V i ​ n , V o ​ u ​ t ) (V_{in},V_{out}) , where V i ​ n = { v i ​ n : v ∈ V } V_{in}=\{v_{in}:v\in V\} and V o ​ u ​ t = { v o ​ u ​ t : v ∈ V } V_{out}=\{v_{out}:v\in V\} , and the only arcs from V i ​ n V_{in} to V o ​ u ​ t V_{out} is the perfect matching { v i ​ n ​ v o ​ u ​ t : v ∈ V } \{v_{in}v_{out}:v\in V\} . Observe that every edge u ​ v uv of G G corresponds to the directed 4-cycle ( u i ​ n , u o ​ u ​ t , v i ​ n , v o ​ u ​ t ) (u_{in},u_{out},v_{in},v_{out}) and that the only induced cycles of D ⁡ ( G ) D(G) are 4-cycles of the form above. See Figure 1 for an example. (a) x x y y z z (b) x 1 x_{1} y 1 y_{1} z 1 z_{1} x 2 x_{2} y 2 y_{2} z 2 z_{2} (c) u u v v x x y y z z (d) u 1 u_{1} v 1 v_{1} x 1 x_{1} y 1 y_{1} z 1 z_{1} u 2 u_{2} v 2 v_{2} x 2 x_{2} y 2 y_{2} z 2 z_{2} Figure 1: Figures (b) and (d) illustrate the digraph D ⁡ ( G ) D(G) for the two graphs shown in Figures (a) and (c), respectively. It turns out that χ → a ​ ( D ​ ( G ) ) \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D(G)) grows with χ ⁡ ( G ) \chi(G) . Proposition 1 . For every graph G G , χ → a ​ ( D ⁡ ( G ) ) = ⌈ χ ⁡ ( G ) ⌉ \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D(G))=\left\lceil\sqrt{\chi(G)}\right\rceil . Proof. Let k = χ → a ​ ( D ​ ( G ) ) k=\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D(G)) . We first prove that k ⩾ ⌈ χ ⁡ ( G ) ⌉ k\geqslant\lceil\sqrt{\chi(G)}\rceil . To see this, let φ : V ⁡ ( D ⁡ ( G ) ) → [ k ] \varphi\colon V(D(G))\rightarrow[k] be an acyclic dicolouring of D ⁡ ( G ) D(G) and assign each vertex v ∈ V ⁡ ( G ) v\in V(G) the colour ψ ⁡ ( v ) = ( φ ⁡ ( v i ​ n ) , φ ⁡ ( v o ​ u ​ t ) ) \psi(v)=(\varphi(v_{in}),\varphi(v_{out})) . We claim that ψ \psi is a proper colouring of G G . To see this, it suffices to see that if u ​ v ∈ E uv\in E is an edge of G G such that ψ ⁡ ( u ) = ψ ⁡ ( v ) \psi(u)=\psi(v) , then either all vertices of the 4-cycle ( u i ​ n , u o ​ u ​ t , v i ​ n , v o ​ u ​ t ) (u_{in},u_{out},v_{in},v_{out}) received the same colour i ∈ [ k ] i\in[k] by φ \varphi or the vertices u i ​ n , v i ​ n u_{in},v_{in} received one colour i i under φ \varphi and u o ​ u ​ t , v o ​ u ​ t u_{out},v_{out} received another colour j j under φ \varphi . In the first case φ − 1 ​ ( i ) \varphi^{-1}(i) is not acyclic and in the second case the 4-cycle ( u i ​ n , u o ​ u ​ t , v i ​ n , v o ​ u ​ t ) (u_{in},u_{out},v_{in},v_{out}) alternates between the sets φ − 1 ​ ( i ) \varphi^{-1}(i) and φ − 1 ​ ( j ) \varphi^{-1}(j) and both cases contradict that φ \varphi is an acyclic dicolouring of D ⁡ ( G ) D(G) . Since ψ \psi uses k 2 k^{2} colours, this shows that k ⩾ χ ⁡ ( G ) k\geqslant\sqrt{\chi(G)} , and since k k is an integer we have k ⩾ ⌈ χ ⁡ ( G ) ⌉ k\geqslant\lceil\sqrt{\chi(G)}\rceil . Let us now show that k ⩽ ⌈ χ ⁡ ( G ) ⌉ k\leqslant\lceil\sqrt{\chi(G)}\rceil . For this, let ℓ \ell be the smallest square number with ℓ ⩾ χ ⁡ ( G ) \ell\geqslant\chi(G) , so we want to show that k ⩽ ℓ k\leqslant\sqrt{\ell} . Suppose that ψ : V ⁡ ( G ) → [ ℓ ] \psi:V(G)\rightarrow[\ell] is a proper ℓ \ell -colouring of G G . We take an arbitrary bijection ρ \rho between the set { 1 , 2 , … , ℓ } \{1,2,\ldots{},\ell\} and the set { ( 1 , 1 ) , … , ( 1 , ℓ ) , ( 2 , 1 ) , … , ( 2 , ℓ ) , … ​ ( ℓ , ℓ ) } \{(1,1),\ldots{},(1,\sqrt{\ell}),(2,1),\ldots{},(2,\sqrt{\ell}),\ldots{}(\sqrt{\ell},\sqrt{\ell})\} , and assign the colours i , j i,j to the vertices v i ​ n , v o ​ u ​ t v_{in},v_{out} if ρ ⁡ ( ψ ⁡ ( v ) ) = ( i , j ) \rho(\psi(v))=(i,j) . To see that φ \varphi is an acyclic dicolouring of D ⁡ ( G ) D(G) , it suffices to consider induced directed cycles of D ⁡ ( G ) D(G) which, by the remark above, are all 4-cycles of the form C = ( u i ​ n , u o ​ u ​ t , v i ​ n , v o ​ u ​ t ) C=(u_{in},u_{out},v_{in},v_{out}) for some pair u , v ∈ V u,v\in V , and such a cycle corresponds to the edge u ​ v uv of G G . As ψ \psi is a proper colouring of G G , it follows from the definition of φ \varphi above that φ \varphi cannot assign the same colour to all four vertices of C C , so each colour class is indeed acyclic. Suppose finally that C C alternates between φ − 1 ​ ( i ) \varphi^{-1}(i) and φ − 1 ​ ( j ) \varphi^{-1}(j) . Then the definition of φ \varphi implies that we have ψ ⁡ ( u ) = ψ ⁡ ( v ) \psi(u)=\psi(v) , contradicting that ψ \psi is a proper colouring of G G . ∎ It follows from Proposition 1 that there exist bipartite tournaments ( i.e. orientations of bipartite complete graphs) with arbitrarily high acyclic dichromatic number, and trivially all bipartite digraphs have dichromatic number at most 2. The same thing holds for tournaments by completing both parts of the partition into a transitive tournament. A transitive tournament is an acyclic tournament, and the unique (up to isomorphism) transitive tournament of order n n is denoted T ​ T n TT_{n} . Proposition 2 . For every integer k k there exists a tournament T T with χ → ​ ( T ) = 2 \vec{\chi}{(T)=2} , χ → a ​ ( T ) ⩾ k \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}{(T)}\geqslant k and such that T T has a spanning acyclic bipartite subdigraph. Proof. Let B = ( V 1 , V 2 , A ) B=(V_{1},V_{2},A) be a bipartite tournament with χ → a ​ ( B ) ⩾ k \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}{(B)}\geqslant k , which exists by Proposition 1 (take for instance D ⁡ ( K k 2 ) D(K_{k^{2}}) ), and form the tournament T T from two copies B 1 , B 2 B_{1},B_{2} of B B with vertex sets V 1 , 1 ∪ V 1 , 2 , V 2 , 1 ∪ V 2 , 2 V_{1,1}\cup{}V_{1,2},V_{2,1}\cup{}V_{2,2} respectively by adding the arcs of a transitive tournament inside each V i , j V_{i,j} and all arcs from V 1 , 1 ∪ V 1 , 2 V_{1,1}\cup{}V_{1,2} to V 2 , 1 ∪ V 2 , 2 V_{2,1}\cup{}V_{2,2} . The partition W 1 = V 1 , 1 ∪ V 2 , 1 W_{1}=V_{1,1}\cup{}V_{2,1} , W 2 = V 2 , 1 ∪ V 2 , 2 W_{2}=V_{2,1}\cup{}V_{2,2} shows that χ → ​ ( T ) = 2 \vec{\chi}(T)=2 and the fact that B B is a subdigraph of T T shows that χ → a ​ ( T ) ⩾ k \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}{(T)}\geqslant k . Moreover, T T has a spanning acyclic bipartite tournament induced by the arcs between V 1 , 1 ∪ V 1 , 2 V_{1,1}\cup{}V_{1,2} and V 2 , 1 ∪ V 2 , 2 V_{2,1}\cup{}V_{2,2} . ∎ This shows that having dichromatic number 2 and a spanning acyclic bipartite subdigraph does not imply that the acyclic dichromatic number is bounded, even for tournaments. We pose the following problem. Problem 3 . What is the complexity of deciding for given numbers k 1 , k 2 ⩾ 1 k_{1},k_{2}\geqslant 1 whether a tournament T T contains a spanning acyclic bipartite tournament B = ( V 1 , V 2 , A ) B=(V_{1},V_{2},A) where | V i | ⩾ k i |V_{i}|\geqslant k_{i} for i = 1 , 2 i=1,2 ? A number of problems concerning the existence of spanning bipartite subdigraphs have been considered in [ 6 ] . 3 Acyclic heroes of tournaments Let T T and H H be two tournaments. We say that T T is H H -free if T T does not contain any copy of H H as a subtournament. The class of H H -free tournaments is denoted by 𝔉 ⁡ ( H ) \mathfrak{F}(H) . The tournament H H is a hero if there exists a constant c H c_{H} such that every tournament T ∈ 𝔉 ⁡ ( H ) T\in\mathfrak{F}(H) has dichromatic number at most c H c_{H} . Berger et al. [ 10 ] obtained an exact characterisation of heroes, for which we first need a few specific definitions. Let H 1 , H 2 , H 3 H_{1},H_{2},H_{3} be three tournaments. We denote by H 1 ⇒ H 2 H_{1}\Rightarrow H_{2} the tournament obtained from disjoint copies of H 1 H_{1} and H 2 H_{2} by putting all the arcs from V ⁡ ( H 1 ) V(H_{1}) to V ⁡ ( H 2 ) V(H_{2}) . We further denote by Δ ⁡ ( H 1 , H 2 , H 3 ) \Delta(H_{1},H_{2},H_{3}) the tournament obtained from disjoint copies of H 1 , H 2 , H 3 H_{1},H_{2},H_{3} by putting all the arcs from V ⁡ ( H 1 ) V(H_{1}) to V ⁡ ( H 2 ) V(H_{2}) , from V ⁡ ( H 2 ) V(H_{2}) to V ⁡ ( H 3 ) V(H_{3}) , and from V ⁡ ( H 3 ) V(H_{3}) to V ⁡ ( H 1 ) V(H_{1}) . For the sake of better readability, in the Δ \Delta operator, we write k k for the transitive tournament T ​ T k TT_{k} , so for instance Δ ⁡ ( H , 1 , k ) \Delta(H,1,k) denotes Δ ⁡ ( H , T ​ T 1 , T ​ T k ) \Delta(H,TT_{1},TT_{k}) . We let ℋ \mathcal{H} be the smallest class of tournaments such that: ( i ) (i) ∅ , T ​ T 1 ∈ ℋ \varnothing,TT_{1}\in\mathcal{H} , ( i ​ i ) (ii) for every H 1 , H 2 ∈ ℋ H_{1},H_{2}\in\mathcal{H} , the tournament H 1 ⇒ H 2 H_{1}\Rightarrow H_{2} belongs to ℋ \mathcal{H} , and ( i ​ i ​ i ) (iii) for every H ∈ ℋ H\in\mathcal{H} and k ⩾ 1 k\geqslant 1 , both tournaments Δ ⁡ ( H , k , 1 ) \Delta(H,k,1) and Δ ⁡ ( H , 1 , k ) \Delta(H,1,k) belong to ℋ \mathcal{H} . It turns out that ℋ \mathcal{H} is precisely the class of heroes. Theorem 4 (Berger et al. [ 10 ] ) . A tournament H H is a hero if and only if H ∈ ℋ H\in\mathcal{H} . Similarly, a tournament H H is an acyclic hero if there exists an absolute constant c H c_{H} such that every tournament T ∈ 𝔉 ⁡ ( H ) T\in\mathfrak{F}(H) has acyclic dichromatic number at most c H c_{H} . Since χ → a ​ ( T ) ⩽ χ → ​ ( T ) \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant\vec{\chi}(T) holds for every tournament T T , observe that, if H H is an acyclic hero, then it is also a hero, but the converse does not hold in general. For every k k , let H k H_{k} be the tournament T ​ T k ⇒ ( Δ ⁡ ( k , 1 , 1 ) ⇒ T ​ T k ) TT_{k}\Rightarrow(\Delta(k,1,1)\Rightarrow TT_{k}) . Let 𝒜 ​ ℋ \mathcal{AH} be the class of all tournaments appearing as a subtournament of H k H_{k} for some integer k k . We pose the following conjecture. Conjecture 5 . A tournament H H is an acyclic hero if and only if H ∈ 𝒜 ​ ℋ H\in\mathcal{AH} . As evidences for the conjecture, we prove the following partial results in the next subsections. Theorem 6 . If H H is an acyclic hero, then H ∈ 𝒜 ​ ℋ H\in\mathcal{AH} . Theorem 7 . The tournament C 3 → ⇒ T ​ T 1 \vec{C_{3}}\Rightarrow TT_{1} is an acyclic hero. Theorem 8 . For every integer k k , Δ ⁡ ( k , 1 , 1 ) \Delta(k,1,1) is an acyclic hero. With Theorem 7 in hands, the following is a natural direction towards the resolution of Conjecture 5 . Harutyunyan, Le, Thomassé, and Wu [ 20 ] proved the following “local to global” property for tournaments. Theorem 9 (Harutyunyan et al. [ 20 ] ) . There exists a function f : ℕ → ℕ f\colon\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N} such that every tournament T T satisfies χ → ​ ( T ) ⩽ max v ∈ V ⁡ ( T ) ⁡ f ⁡ ( χ → ​ ( T ⁡ [ N + ​ ( v ) ] ) ) . \vec{\chi}(T)\leqslant\max_{v\in V(T)}f\Bigg(\vec{\chi}\Big(T[N^{+}(v)]\Big)\Bigg). We believe that the same property holds for the acyclic dichromatic number, and pose the following conjecture. Conjecture 10 . There exists a function f : ℕ → ℕ f\colon\mathbb{N}\to\mathbb{N} such that every tournament T T satisfies χ → a ​ ( T ) ⩽ max v ∈ V ⁡ ( T ) ⁡ f ⁡ ( χ → a ​ ( T ⁡ [ N + ​ ( v ) ] ) ) . \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant\max_{v\in V(T)}f\Bigg(\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}\Big(T[N^{+}(v)]\Big)\Bigg). An easy inductive argument shows that, if true, Conjecture 10 together with Theorem 8 imply Conjecture 5 . 3.1 Proof of Theorem 6 Lemma 11 . The tournaments C → 3 ⇒ C