=1 and H H is isomorphic to Δ ⁡ ( max ⁡ ( k , k ′ ) , 1 , 1 ) \Delta(\max(k,k^{\prime}),1,1) . The result follows. ∎ 3.2 Proof of Theorem 7 The proof of Theorem 7 relies on the particular structure of prime heroes. Given a tournament T T , a homogeneous set is a set of vertices X X such that, for every vertex u ∈ V ⁡ ( T ) ∖ X u\in V(T)\setminus X , either X ⊆ N − ​ ( u ) X\subseteq N^{-}(u) or X ⊆ N + ​ ( u ) X\subseteq N^{+}(u) . The set X X is further nontrivial if 2 ⩽ | X | ⩽ | V ⁡ ( T ) | − 1 2\leqslant|X|\leqslant|V(T)|-1 . A tournament is prime if it does not contain any nontrivial homogeneous set. For an odd integer n n , we define R n R_{n} as the tournament with vertex set V ⁡ ( R n ) = { v 0 , … , v n − 1 } V(R_{n})=\{v_{0},\dots,v_{n-1}\} and arc set A ⁡ ( R n ) = { v i ​ v i + j : 0 ⩽ i ⩽ n − 1 ​ and ​ 1 ⩽ j ⩽ n − 1 2 } , A(R_{n})=\left\{v_{i}v_{i+j}:0\leqslant i\leqslant n-1\mbox{ and }1\leqslant j\leqslant\tfrac{n-1}{2}\right\}, where indices are taken modulo n n . Liu [ 24 , Theorem 3.8] obtained the following result, from which we derive Theorem 7 . Theorem 12 (Liu [ 24 ] ) . Let T T be a prime tournament. If n = | V ⁡ ( T ) | ⩾ 3 n=|V(T)|\geqslant 3 and T ∈ 𝔉 ⁡ ( C 3 → ⇒ T ​ T 1 ) T\in\mathfrak{F}(\vec{C_{3}}\Rightarrow TT_{1}) , then n n is odd and T T is isomorphic to R n R_{n} . Proof of Theorem 7 . We prove the stronger result that every tournament T ∈ 𝔉 ⁡ ( C 3 → ⇒ T ​ T 1 ) T\in\mathfrak{F}(\vec{C_{3}}\Rightarrow TT_{1}) satisfies χ → a ​ ( T ) ⩽ 2 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant 2 . For the sake of contradiction, let T T be a minimum counterexample, so χ → a ​ ( T ) ⩾ 3 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\geqslant 3 and χ → a ​ ( T ′ ) ⩽ 2 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T^{\prime})\leqslant 2 for every tournament T ′ ∈ 𝔉 ⁡ ( C 3 → ⇒ T ​ T 1 ) T^{\prime}\in\mathfrak{F}(\vec{C_{3}}\Rightarrow TT_{1}) of smaller order. By minimality, T T is strongly connected, otherwise we can 2 2 -colour each strongly connected component independently. We further claim that T T must be prime. Otherwise, let X X be a non-trivial homogeneous set. If X X contains a directed triangle, since T T is connected then T T contains a vertex u u with X ⊆ N − ​ ( u ) X\subseteq N^{-}(u) , and T T contains a subtournament isomorphic to C 3 → ⇒ T ​ T 1 \vec{C_{3}}\Rightarrow TT_{1} , a contradiction. Hence, T ⁡ [ X ] T[X] is transitive. Let T ′ T^{\prime} be the tournament obtained from T T by contracting X X into a single vertex x ⋆ x^{\star} . By minimality of T T , T ′ T^{\prime} admits an acyclic 2 2 -dicolouring φ \varphi . It is straightforward to check that φ \varphi extends from T ′ T^{\prime} to T T by giving colour φ ⁡ ( x ⋆ ) \varphi(x^{\star}) to all the vertices in X X . Hence, T T is prime, | V ⁡ ( T ) | ⩾ 3 |V(T)|\geqslant 3 , and T ∈ 𝔉 ⁡ ( C 3 → ⇒ T ​ T 1 ) T\in\mathfrak{F}(\vec{C_{3}}\Rightarrow TT_{1}) . By Theorem 12 , n = | V ⁡ ( T ) | n=|V(T)| is odd and T T is isomorphic to R n R_{n} . It is straightforward to check that giving vertices v 0 , … , v ( n − 1 ) / 2 v_{0},\dots,v_{(n-1)/2} colour 1 1 and vertices v ( n + 1 ) / 2 , … , v n − 1 v_{(n+1)/2},\dots,v_{n-1} colour 2 2 yields an acyclic 2 2 -dicolouring of R n R_{n} , hence yielding the contradiction. ∎ 3.3 Proof of Theorem 8 We finally show that Δ ⁡ ( k , 1 , 1 ) \Delta(k,1,1) is an acyclic hero. We first show that it is an acyclic hero in the class of 2 2 -dicolourable tournaments, and then combine it with the fact that it is a hero to obtain the result. We make use of the following celebrated result, obtained in 1964 by Erdős and Moser [ 16 ] . Theorem 13 . For every integer k k , every tournament of order at least 2 k − 1 2^{k-1} contains T ​ T k TT_{k} . Lemma 14 . For every k ⩾ 1 k\geqslant 1 and T ∈ 𝔉 ⁡ ( Δ ⁡ ( k , 1 , 1 ) ) T\in\mathfrak{F}(\Delta(k,1,1)) , if T T satisfies χ → ​ ( T ) ⩽ 2 \vec{\chi}(T)\leqslant 2 then χ → a ​ ( T ) ⩽ 2 ⋅ 4 k \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant 2\cdot 4^{k} . Proof. We prove by induction that, for any tournament T ∈ 𝔉 ⁡ ( Δ ⁡ ( k , 1 , 1 ) ) T\in\mathfrak{F}(\Delta(k,1,1)) and any partition of V ⁡ ( T ) V(T) into two sets ( V 1 , V 2 ) (V_{1},V_{2}) with T ⁡ [ V 1 ] T[V_{1}] and T ⁡ [ V 2 ] T[V_{2}] being acyclic, there exists an acyclic dicolouring of T T using at most 4 k 4^{k} colours on V 1 V_{1} and at most 4 k 4^{k} colours on V 2 V_{2} , and that these two sets of colours are disjoint. Observe first that if one of V 1 V_{1} or V 2 V_{2} is empty, then the result is trivial, as we may colour every vertex of the other set with the same colour. Therefore, assume that V 1 ≠ ∅ V_{1}\neq\varnothing and V 2 ≠ ∅ V_{2}\neq\varnothing . Let t 1 t_{1} and t 2 t_{2} be the sinks of T ⁡ [ V 1 ] T[V_{1}] and T ⁡ [ V 2 ] T[V_{2}] , respectively. We assume by symmetry that the arc between t 1 t_{1} and t 2 t_{2} goes from t 2 t_{2} to t 1 t_{1} . Let T ′ = T − t 1 T^{\prime}=T-t_{1} , and assume by induction that φ \varphi is an acyclic dicolouring of T ′ T^{\prime} , where φ ⁡ ( V 1 ∖ { t 1 } ) ⊆ { 1 , … , 4 k } \varphi(V_{1}\setminus\{t_{1}\})\subseteq\{1,\dots,4^{k}\} and φ ⁡ ( V 2 ) ⊆ { 4 k + 1 , … , 2 ⋅ 4 k } \varphi(V_{2})\subseteq\{4^{k}+1,\dots,2\cdot 4^{k}\} . We claim that φ \varphi can be extended to T T by choosing for t 1 t_{1} a colour from { 1 , … , 4 k } \{1,\dots,4^{k}\} that is not given by φ \varphi to any vertex in ⋃ u ∈ N + ​ ( t 1 ) N + ​ ( u ) . \bigcup_{u\in N^{+}(t_{1})}N^{+}(u). Assume first that such a colour exists, and let us briefly justify that the obtained extension of φ \varphi is indeed an acyclic dicolouring. If this is not the case, then there exists a directed cycle 𝒞 \mathscr{C} which is either monochromatic or alternating between two colour classes. Among all such cycles, we let 𝒞 \mathscr{C} be a shortest one, so it has length either 3 3 or 4 4 (as T T is a tournament). By the induction hypothesis, 𝒞 \mathscr{C} contains t 1 t_{1} . Moreover, since T ⁡ [ V 1 ] T[V_{1}] is acyclic, 𝒞 \mathscr{C} contains at least one vertex of V 2 V_{2} , and because vertices in V 1 V_{1} and V 2 V_{2} use distinct sets of colours, 𝒞 \mathscr{C} is necessarily a directed 4 4 -cycle ( t 1 , x , y , z ) (t_{1},x,y,z) alternating between two colour classes. We conclude that φ ⁡ ( t 1 ) = φ ⁡ ( y ) \varphi(t_{1})=\varphi(y) , a contradiction, since y ∈ ⋃ u ∈ N + ​ ( t 1 ) N + ​ ( u ) y\in\bigcup_{u\in N^{+}(t_{1})}N^{+}(u) . We now argue that such a colour exists. Recall that N + ​ ( t 1 ) ⊆ V 2 N^{+}(t_{1})\subseteq V_{2} , and that N + ​ ( t 1 ) ⊆ N − ​ ( t 2 ) N^{+}(t_{1})\subseteq N^{-}(t_{2}) , since t 1 t_{1} and t 2 t_{2} are sinks in T ⁡ [ V 1 ] T[V_{1}] and T ⁡ [ V 2 ] T[V_{2}] respectively. Therefore, we have d + ​ ( t 1 ) ⩽ 2 k − 1 d^{+}(t_{1})\leqslant 2^{k-1} , for otherwise, by Theorem 13 , T ⁡ [ N + ​ ( t 1 ) ∩ N − ​ ( t 2 ) ] T[N^{+}(t_{1})\cap N^{-}(t_{2})] contains a copy of T ​ T k TT_{k} , contradicting the assumption that T ∈ 𝔉 ⁡ ( Δ ⁡ ( k , 1 , 1 ) ) T\in\mathfrak{F}(\Delta(k,1,1)) . Now, let us fix a vertex u ∈ N + ​ ( t 1 ) u\in N^{+}(t_{1}) . The same argument, relying on the fact that t 1 t_{1} is a sink in T ⁡ [ V 1 ] T[V_{1}] , shows that | N + ​ ( u ) ∩ V 1 | ⩽ 2 k − 1 |N^{+}(u)\cap V_{1}|\leqslant 2^{k-1} . It follows that ⋃ u ∈ N + ​ ( t 1 ) N + ​ ( u ) \bigcup_{u\in N^{+}(t_{1})}N^{+}(u) contains at most 2 2 ​ k − 2 ⩽ 4 k − 1 2^{2k-2}\leqslant 4^{k}-1 vertices of V 1 V_{1} . Since vertices of V 2 V_{2} use colours distinct from { 1 , … , 4 k } \{1,\dots,4^{k}\} , it follows that one colour is available for t 1 t_{1} , showing the result. ∎ Lemma 15 . Let H H be a hero such that, for some c ∈ ℕ c\in\mathbb{N} , every H H -free tournament T T with χ → ​ ( T ) ⩽ 2 \vec{\chi}(T)\leqslant 2 satisfies χ → a ​ ( T ) ⩽ c \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant c . Then H H is an acyclic hero. Proof. Let T T be an arbitrary H H -free tournament, and let us show that it has bounded acyclic dichromatic number. As H H is a hero, there exists k k such that χ → ​ ( T ) ⩽ k \vec{\chi}(T)\leqslant k . We fix V 1 , … , V k V_{1},\dots,V_{k} a partition of V ⁡ ( T ) V(T) into transitive tournaments. For every 1 ⩽ i < j ⩽ k 1\leqslant i<j\leqslant k , we let φ i , j \varphi_{i,j} be an acyclic dicolouring of T ⁡ [ V i ∪ V j ] T[V_{i}\cup V_{j}] using at most c c colours, which exists by assumption. For every i ∈ [ 1 , k ] i\in[1,k] and every vertex u ∈ V i u\in V_{i} , we let ψ ⁡ ( u ) = ( φ 1 , i ​ ( u ) , φ 2 , i ​ ( u ) , … , φ i − 1 , i ​ ( u ) , 0 , φ i , i + 1 ​ ( u ) , … , φ i , k ​ ( u ) ) . \psi(u)=\Big(\varphi_{1,i}(u),\varphi_{2,i}(u),\dots,\varphi_{i-1,i}(u),0,\varphi_{i,i+1}(u),\dots,\varphi_{i,k}(u)\Big). For any vertex u u and integer i ∈ [ 1 , k ] i\in[1,k] , we further denote by ψ i ​ ( u ) \psi_{i}(u) the i th i^{\rm th} coordinate of ψ ⁡ ( u ) \psi(u) . Note that ψ \psi is a colouring of T T using at most ( c + 1 ) k (c+1)^{k} colours (since every coordinate of ψ ⁡ ( u ) \psi(u) is an integer of [ 0 , c ] [0,c] ). It remains to show that it is indeed an acyclic dicolouring. Assume for a contradiction that T T , coloured with ψ \psi , contains a directed cycle 𝒞 \mathscr{C} which is either monochromatic or alternating between two colour classes of ψ \psi . Among all such cycles, we choose 𝒞 \mathscr{C} of minimum length. Note that, by definition, ψ i ​ ( u ) = 0 \psi_{i}(u)=0 if and only if u ∈ V i u\in V_{i} . In particular, each colour class of ψ \psi is included in some V i V_{i} . It follows that 𝒞 \mathscr{C} cannot be monochromatic, so it is alternating between V i V_{i} and V j V_{j} for some indices 1 ⩽ i < j ⩽ k 1\leqslant i<j\leqslant k , and 𝒞 \mathscr{C} has length 4 4 . Let us denote 𝒞 = ( u , x , v , y ) \mathscr{C}=(u,x,v,y) , where u , v ∈ V i u,v\in V_{i} and x , y ∈ V j x,y\in V_{j} . Since 𝒞 \mathscr{C} alternates between two colour classes of ψ \psi , in particular we have ψ j ​ ( u ) = ψ j ​ ( v ) \psi_{j}(u)=\psi_{j}(v) , which by definition of ψ \psi implies φ i , j ​ ( u ) = φ i , j ​ ( v ) \varphi_{i,j}(u)=\varphi_{i,j}(v) . Similarly, we have φ i , j ​ ( x ) = φ i , j ​ ( y ) \varphi_{i,j}(x)=\varphi_{i,j}(y) . Therefore, 𝒞 \mathscr{C} is a directed cycle in T ⁡ [ V i ∪ V j ] T[V_{i}\cup V_{j}] alternating between two colour classes of φ i , j \varphi_{i,j} , a contradiction. The result follows. ∎ Theorem 8 follows directly from the combination of Lemmas 14 and 15 . 4 Complexity In this section, we study the complexity of the following problem, defined for every fixed integer k k . Acyclic k k -Dicolourability Input: An oriented graph D D . Question: Does D D admit an acyclic k k -dicolouring? Recall that, by Proposition 1 , for any graph G G , one can construct in polynomial time the oriented graph D ⁡ ( G ) D(G) satisfying χ → a ​ ( D ⁡ ( G ) ) = ⌈ χ ⁡ ( G ) ⌉ \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D(G))=\lceil\sqrt{\chi(G)}\rceil . Recall furthermore that D ⁡ ( G ) D(G) is a bipartite digraph. Since, for every integer k ⩾ 2 k\geqslant 2 , it is NP-complete to decide whether a graph is k 2 k^{2} -colourable, we directly obtain the following. Corollary 16 . For every integer k ⩾ 2 k\geqslant 2 , Acyclic k k -Dicolourability is NP-complete even when restricted to bipartite digraphs. In Section 4.1 , we give another reduction showing that Acyclic k k -Dicolourability is NP-hard, for every integer k ⩾ 3 k\geqslant 3 , even on the class of split digraphs. In Section 4.2 , we provide a polynomial-time algorithm for Acyclic 2 2 -Dicolourability on tournaments. 4.1 Complexity for split digraphs A digraph D = ( V , A ) D=(V,A) is a split digraph if we can partition its vertices into two sets V 1 , V 2 V_{1},V_{2} so that D ⁡ [ V 1 ] D[V_{1}] has no arcs and D ⁡ [ V 2 ] D[V_{2}] is a semicomplete digraph. We call V 1 , V 2 V_{1},V_{2} a split partition of V ⁡ ( D ) V(D) . A split digraph D = ( V , A ) D=(V,A) is semicomplete if every vertex in V 1 V_{1} is adjacent to every vertex in V 2 V_{2} . Theorem 17 . For every fixed k ⩾ 3 k\geqslant 3 , Acyclic k k -Dicolouring is NP-complete even when restricted to split digraphs. However, the complexity of the problem remains open when k = 2 k=2 or when the input is a complete split digraph. Problem 18 . What is the complexity of deciding whether a split digraph D D has χ → a ​ ( D ) = 2 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)=2 ? Problem 19 . What is the complexity of deciding whether a semicomplete split digraph D D has χ → a ​ ( D ) = 2 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)=2 ? Note that a semicomplete split digraph is a special case of a semicomplete multipartite digraph in which all but one partite set has size one. Hence, proving the above polynomial would be a step towards determining the complexity for multipartite tournaments. The remainder of this section is devoted to the proof of Theorem 17 . We first prove the hardness for k = 3 k=3 . Lemma 20 . Acyclic 3 3 -Dicolouring is NP-hard even when restricted to split digraphs. Proof. We reduce from 2 2 -Dicolourability , which is well-known to be NP-hard (see [ 11 ] ). Let D D be any instance of this problem. We assume that D D has minimum in-degree at least 1 1 , for otherwise we repeatedly remove sources from D D to obtain an equivalent instance. We build D ⋆ D^{\star} as follows. To every vertex v ∈ V ⁡ ( D ) v\in V(D) we associate a vertex x v x_{v} in D ⋆ D^{\star} , and to every arc a ∈ A ⁡ ( D ) a\in A(D) we associate a vertex x a x_{a} . Furthermore, for every arc a = u ​ v ∈ A ⁡ ( D ) a=uv\in A(D) , we add the arcs x u ​ x a , x a ​ x v x_{u}x_{a},x_{a}x_{v} . We fix an arbitrary ordering a 1 , … , a m a_{1},\dots,a_{m} of A ⁡ ( D ) A(D) , and add to D ⋆ D^{\star} all the arcs x a i ​ x a j x_{a_{i}}x_{a_{j}} with j > i j>i , so X A = { x a : a ∈ A ⁡ ( D ) } X_{A}=\{x_{a}:a\in A(D)\} induces a transitive tournament. We further add a new vertex x b x_{b} dominating X A X_{A} and being dominated by X V = { x v : v ∈ V ⁡ ( D ) } X_{V}=\{x_{v}:v\in V(D)\} We finally add the six vertices in Y = { y 1 , y 2 , y 3 } Y=\{y_{1},y_{2},y_{3}\} and Z = { z 1 , z 2 , z 3 } Z=\{z_{1},z_{2},z_{3}\} , together with the arcs in { y 1 y 2 , y 2 y 3 , y 3 y 1 } ∪ { z 1 z 2 , z 2 z 3 , z 3 z 1 } ∪ { x a y , y z , z x a : a ∈ A ( D ) ∪ { b } , y ∈ Y , z ∈ Z } , \{y_{1}y_{2},y_{2}y_{3},y_{3}y_{1}\}\cup\{z_{1}z_{2},z_{2}z_{3},z_{3}z_{1}\}\cup\{x_{a}y,yz,zx_{a}:a\in A(D)\cup\{b\},y\in Y,z\in Z\}, see Figure 2 for an illustration. t t u u v v w w x t x_{t} x u x_{u} x v x_{v} x w x_{w} x b x_{b} X V X_{V} X A ∪ { x b } X_{A}\cup\{x_{b}\} Y Y Z Z Figure 2: A digraph D D together with one of its 2 2 -dicolourings φ \varphi (left), and the corresponding digraph D ⋆ D^{\star} with one of its acyclic 3 3 -dicolourings (right). By construction, X V X_{V} is an independent set, and D ⋆ − X V D^{\star}-X_{V} is a tournament, so D ⋆ D^{\star} is a split digraph. We now show that χ → ​ ( D ) ⩽ 2 \vec{\chi}(D)\leqslant 2 if and only if χ → a ​ ( D ⋆ ) ⩽ 3 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D^{\star})\leqslant 3 , implying the lemma. Claim 20.1 . If χ → ​ ( D ) ⩽ 2 \vec{\chi}(D)\leqslant 2 then χ → a ​ ( D ⋆ ) ⩽ 3 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D^{\star})\leqslant 3 . Proof of claim . Assume that φ : V ⁡ ( D ) → { 1 , 2 } \varphi\colon V(D)\to\{1,2\} is a 2 2 -dicolouring of D D . Let φ ⋆ : V ⁡ ( D ⋆ ) → { 1 , 2 , 3 } \varphi^{\star}\colon V(D^{\star})\to\{1,2,3\} be the colouring of D ⋆ D^{\star} defined as follows: φ ⋆ ​ ( x ) = { φ ⁡ ( v ) if ​ x = x v ​ with ​ v ∈ V ⁡ ( D ) 3 if ​ x ∈ X A ∪ { x b } 1 if ​ x ∈ { y 1 , z 1 } 2 otherwise, that is if ​ x ∈ { y 2 , y 3 , z 2 , z 3 } . \varphi^{\star}(x)=\left\{\begin{array}[]{ll}\varphi(v)&\mbox{if }x=x_{v}\mbox{ with }v\in V(D)\\ 3&\mbox{if }x\in X_{A}\cup\{x_{b}\}\\ 1&\mbox{if }x\in\{y_{1},z_{1}\}\\ 2&\mbox{otherwise, that is if }x\in\{y_{2},y_{3},z_{2},z_{3}\}.\end{array}\right. We claim that φ ⋆ \varphi^{\star} is an acyclic dicolouring of D ⋆ D^{\star} . Clearly, each colour class induces an acyclic digraph, and the digraph D ⁡ [ V 1 , V 2 ] D[V_{1},V_{2}] is acyclic as well (where V i V_{i} is the set of vertices coloured i i in φ ⋆ \varphi^{\star} ). Therefore, if φ ⋆ \varphi^{\star} is not an acyclic dicolouring, there exists a directed cycle 𝒞 \mathscr{C} alternating between V i V_{i} and V 3 V_{3} for some i ∈ { 1 , 2 } i\in\{1,2\} . Note that 𝒞 \mathscr{C} does not contain any vertex z ∈ Z z\in Z , as every in-neighbour of z z is coloured with { 1 , 2 } \{1,2\} . Similarly, 𝒞 \mathscr{C} does not contain any vertex in Y Y , and it does not contain x b x_{b} . Therefore, 𝒞 \mathscr{C} is included in the digraph D ⁡ [ X V , X A ] D[X_{V},X_{A}] , meaning that 𝒞 \mathscr{C} is of the form 𝒞 = ( x u 1 , x u 1 ​ u 2 , x u 2 , … , x u ℓ − 1 ​ u ℓ , x u ℓ , x u ℓ ​ u 1 ) . \mathscr{C}=(x_{u_{1}},x_{u_{1}u_{2}},x_{u_{2}},\ldots,x_{u_{\ell-1}u_{\ell}},x_{u_{\ell}},x_{u_{\ell}u_{1}}). But then 𝒞 ′ = ( u 1 , u 2 , … , u ℓ ) \mathscr{C}^{\prime}=(u_{1},u_{2},\dots,u_{\ell}) is a monochromatic directed cycle in D D coloured with φ \varphi , a contradiction. ◊ \lozenge Claim 20.2 . If χ → a ​ ( D ⋆ ) ⩽ 3 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D^{\star})\leqslant 3 then χ → ​ ( D ) ⩽ 2 \vec{\chi}(D)\leqslant 2 . Proof of claim . Let φ ⋆ \varphi^{\star} be an acyclic 3 3 -dicolouring of D D with colour classes V 1 , V 2 , V 3 V_{1},V_{2},V_{3} , and assume without loss of generality that x b ∈ V 3 x_{b}\in V_{3} . We first show that X A ∪ { x b } ⊆ V 3 X_{A}\cup\{x_{b}\}\subseteq V_{3} . Assume that this is not the case, and assume by symmetry that x a ∈ V 1 x_{a}\in V_{1} for some a ∈ A ⁡ ( D ) a\in A(D) . Since both Y Y and Z Z induce directed triangles, note that some colour appears on both Y Y and Z Z . If colour 3 3 belongs to φ ⁡ ( Y ) ∩ φ ⁡ ( Z ) \varphi(Y)\cap\varphi(Z) , then ( x b , y , z ) (x_{b},y,z) is a monochromatic directed triangle for some y ∈ Y y\in Y and z ∈ Z z\in Z . Similarly, because of x a x_{a} being coloured 1 1 , colour 1 1 does not belong to φ ⁡ ( Y ) ∩ φ ⁡ ( Z ) \varphi(Y)\cap\varphi(Z) . Therefore, φ ⁡ ( Y ) ∩ φ ⁡ ( Z ) = { 2 } \varphi(Y)\cap\varphi(Z)=\{2\} . If there exists y ∈ Y y\in Y with φ ⁡ ( y ) = 3 \varphi(y)=3 , then there exists z ∈ Z z\in Z with φ ⁡ ( z ) = 1 \varphi(z)=1 , and ( x a , y , z , x b ) (x_{a},y,z,x_{b}) alternates between V 1 V_{1} and V 3 V_{3} , a contradiction. Therefore, φ ⁡ ( Y ) = { 1 , 2 } \varphi(Y)=\{1,2\} and φ ⁡ ( Z ) = { 2 , 3 } \varphi(Z)=\{2,3\} . Assume without loss of generality that φ ⁡ ( y 1 ) = 2 \varphi(y_{1})=2 , φ ⁡ ( z 1 ) = 3 \varphi(z_{1})=3 and φ ⁡ ( z 2 ) = 2 \varphi(z_{2})=2 , then ( x b , y 1 , z 1 , z 2 ) (x_{b},y_{1},z_{1},z_{2}) alternates between V 2 V_{2} and V 3 V_{3} , a contradiction. This shows X A ∪ { x b } ⊆ V 3 X_{A}\cup\{x_{b}\}\subseteq V_{3} . It follows that V 3 ∩ X V = ∅ V_{3}\cap X_{V}=\varnothing . Indeed, if some vertex x v ∈ X V x_{v}\in X_{V} is coloured 3 3 , then for any entering arc a a of v v in D D (recall that D D has minimum in-degree at least 1 1 ), then the 3-cycle ( x b , x a , x v ) (x_{b},x_{a},x_{v}) is monochromatic, a contradiction. This allows us to define φ : V ⁡ ( D ) → { 1 , 2 } \varphi\colon V(D)\to\{1,2\} , the 2 2 -colouring of D D where, for every v ∈ V ⁡ ( D ) v\in V(D) , φ ⁡ ( v ) = φ ⋆ ​ ( x v ) . \varphi(v)=\varphi^{\star}(x_{v}). We claim that φ \varphi is a 2 2 -dicolouring of D D . Indeed, if 𝒞 = ( u 1 , u 2 , … , u ℓ ) \mathscr{C}=(u_{1},u_{2},\dots,u_{\ell}) is a monochromatic directed cycle in D D coloured with φ \varphi , then 𝒞 ⋆ = ( x u 1 , x u 1 ​ u 2 , x u 2 , … , x u ℓ − 1 ​ u ℓ , x u ℓ , x u ℓ ​ u 1 ) \mathscr{C}^{\star}=(x_{u_{1}},x_{u_{1}u_{2}},x_{u_{2}},\ldots,x_{u_{\ell-1}u_{\ell}},x_{u_{\ell}},x_{u_{\ell}u_{1}}) is a directed cycle in D ⋆ D^{\star} alternating between two colour classes of φ ⋆ \varphi^{\star} , a contradiction. ◊ \lozenge The lemma follows. ∎ We now derive Theorem 17 . Proof of Theorem 17 . It is clear that Acyclic k k -Dicolourability is in NP. We prove by induction on k ⩾ 3 k\geqslant 3 that it is NP-hard for every k ⩾ 3 k\geqslant 3 , even when restricted to split digraphs, the case k = 3 k=3 corresponding exactly to Lemma 20 . Let D k D_{k} be any digraph and T k T_{k} be a tournament with acyclic dichromatic number k k , whose size depends only on k k ( e.g. the tournament constructed in the proof of Proposition 2 , which has χ → a ⩾ k \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}\geqslant k so contains a subtournament, T k T_{k} , with χ → a ​ ( T k ) = k \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T_{k})=k ). We let D k + 1 D_{k+1} be the digraph obtained by taking disjoint copies D k D_{k} and T k T_{k} with one extra vertex v v and putting all arcs in { v u , u t , t v : u ∈ V ( D k ) , t ∈ V ( T k ) } . \{vu,ut,tv:u\in V(D_{k}),t\in V(T_{k})\}. Note that the size of D k + 1 D_{k+1} is at most a polynomial in the size of D k D_{k} ( k k being fixed), and that if D k D_{k} is a split digraph then so is D k + 1 D_{k+1} . The hardness of Acyclic ( k + 1 ) (k+1) -Dicolourability for split digraphs thus follows from the hardness of Acyclic k k -Dicolourability for split digraphs together with the following claim. Note that we may assume that D k D_{k} is strongly connected, as when computing the acyclic dicolourability of a digraph, it suffices to do so for each of the strong components (and take the maximum over all strong components). Claim 20.3 . We have χ → a ​ ( D k ) ⩽ k \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D_{k})\leqslant k if and only if χ → a ​ ( D k + 1 ) ⩽ k + 1 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D_{k+1})\leqslant k+1 . Proof of claim . Assume first that χ → a ​ ( D k ) ⩽ k \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D_{k})\leqslant k , and let ψ \psi be the ( k + 1 ) (k+1) -colouring of D k + 1 D_{k+1} obtained by taking an acyclic k k -dicolouring of D k D_{k} , an acyclic k k -dicolouring of T k T_{k} , and using colour k + 1 k+1 for v v . Assume for a contradiction that ψ \psi is not an acyclic dicolouring of D k + 1 D_{k+1} , that is D k + 1 D_{k+1} contains a directed cycle 𝒞 \mathscr{C} which is either monochromatic or alternating between two colours. Then 𝒞 \mathscr{C} does not contain v v , as v v is the only vertex coloured k + 1 k+1 . Hence, 𝒞 \mathscr{C} is included in a strongly connected component of D k + 1 − v D_{k+1}-v , so in particular 𝒞 \mathscr{C} is included either in D k D_{k} or in T k T_{k} , a contradiction. Assume now that χ → a ​ ( D k + 1 ) ⩽ k + 1 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D_{k+1})\leqslant k+1 and let ψ \psi be an acyclic ( k + 1 ) (k+1) -dicolouring of D k + 1 D_{k+1} . We assume without loss of generality that v v is coloured k + 1 k+1 . If both T k T_{k} and D k D_{k} use colour k + 1 k+1 , then D k + 1 D_{k+1} contains a monochromatic directed triangle, a contradiction. Therefore, one of them does not use colour k + 1 k+1 . If D k D_{k} does not use colour k + 1 k+1 , then ψ \psi uses at most k k colours on D k D_{k} and the result follows, so we assume that ( k + 1 ) ∉ ψ ⁡ ( V ⁡ ( T k ) ) (k+1)\notin\psi(V(T_{k})) . Since χ → a ​ ( T k ) = k \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T_{k})=k , for every i ∈ [ 1 , k ] i\in[1,k] there exists a vertex v i v_{i} coloured i i in T k T_{k} . Let 𝒰 \mathcal{U} be the set of vertices coloured k + 1 k+1 in D k D_{k} . Clearly, we may assume that 𝒰 \mathcal{U} has at least one entering arc in D k D_{k} , for otherwise D k D_{k} would not be strongly connected. Hence, let x ​ y ∈ A ⁡ ( D k ) xy\in A(D_{k}) be such that ψ ⁡ ( x ) ≠ k + 1 \psi(x)\neq k+1 and ψ ⁡ ( y ) = k + 1 \psi(y)=k+1 . Now there exists z ∈ V ⁡ ( T k ) z\in V(T_{k}) with ψ ⁡ ( z ) = ψ ⁡ ( x ) \psi(z)=\psi(x) , and ( v , x , y , z ) (v,x,y,z) is a directed cycle alternating between colour classes k + 1 k+1 and ψ ⁡ ( x ) \psi(x) , a contradiction. ◊ \lozenge The result follows. ∎ 4.2 A polynomial-time algorithm for Acyclic 2 2 -Dicolouring on tournaments We proved in the last sections that deciding χ → a ​ ( D ) ⩽ 2 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant 2 is NP-hard for general oriented graphs D D . We now show that the situation is different for tournaments. This also draws a distinction with the 2 2 -Dicolourability problem, which is well-known to be NP-hard even when restricted to tournaments [ 14 ] (see also [ 8 , Theorem 2.8.21] for a shorter proof). Our proof is inspired by a proof due to Klingelhoefer and Newman [ 23 ] that a 10 10 -dicolouring of a tournament T T can be computed in polynomial time when T T is guaranteed to have dichromatic number 2 2 . Precisely, we make use of a decomposition of 2 2 -dicolourable light tournaments that they obtained. A tournament T T is light if, for every arc u ​ v ∈ A ⁡ ( T ) uv\in A(T) , the subtournament T ⁡ [ N + ​ ( v ) ∩ N − ​ ( u ) ] T[N^{+}(v)\cap N^{-}(u)] is acyclic. Theorem 21 . There exists a polynomial-time algorithm for deciding whether a given tournament T T admits an acyclic 2 2 -dicolouring, and computing one when it exists. The cases of more than two colours remain open. We believe that Acyclic k k -Dicolouring , when restricted to tournaments, is actually solvable in polynomial time for every fixed k k . We pose the following stronger conjecture. A tournament T T is k k -critical if χ → a ​ ( T ) = k \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)=k and every proper subtournament H H of T T satisfies χ → a ​ ( H ) ⩽ k − 1 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(H)\leqslant k-1 . Conjecture 22 . For every k ∈ ℕ k\in\mathbb{N} , there is a finite number of k k -critical tournaments. If the conjecture above is true, then in particular there is a trivial polynomial time algorithm for deciding χ → a ​ ( T ) ⩽ k \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant k , consisting of checking whether T T contains one of the ( k + 1 ) (k+1) -critical tournaments. The conjecture remains open for every k ⩾ 2 k\geqslant 2 . The remainder of this subsection is devoted to the proof of Theorem 21 . Proof of Theorem 21 . Let T T be any tournament on n ⩾ 3 n\geqslant 3 vertices (the algorithm being trivial when n ⩽ 2 n\leqslant 2 ). Let us describe the claimed algorithm. Clearly, we may assume that T T is strongly connected, for otherwise we apply the following algorithm to each strongly connected component of T T . We first justify that we can further assume that T T is light. Claim 0.1 . If χ → a ​ ( T ) ⩽ 2 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant 2 then T T is light. Proof of claim . Assume that χ → a ​ ( T ) ⩽ 2 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant 2 and let φ \varphi be an acyclic 2 2 -dicolouring of T T . Assume for a contradiction that T T is not light, so there exists an arc u ​ v ∈ A ⁡ ( T ) uv\in A(T) such that T ⁡ [ N + ​ ( v ) ∩ N − ​ ( u ) ] T[N^{+}(v)\cap N^{-}(u)] contains a directed triangle ( x , y , z ) (x,y,z) . Assume first that φ ⁡ ( u ) = φ ⁡ ( v ) \varphi(u)=\varphi(v) . Since ( x , y , z ) (x,y,z) is a directed triangle, one of these three vertices, say x x , uses colour φ ⁡ ( u ) \varphi(u) . Therefore, ( v , x , u ) (v,x,u) is a monochromatic directed triangle, a contradiction. Assume now, without loss of generality, that φ ⁡ ( u ) = 1 \varphi(u)=1 and φ ⁡ ( v ) = 2 \varphi(v)=2 . Since φ \varphi uses both colours 1 1 and 2 2 on { x , y , z } \{x,y,z\} (otherwise ( x , y , z ) (x,y,z) is monochromatic), there exists an arc, say x ​ y xy , such that φ ⁡ ( x ) = 1 \varphi(x)=1 and φ ⁡ ( y ) = 2 \varphi(y)=2 . Therefore, ( v , x , y , u ) (v,x,y,u) is a directed cycle alternating between colours 1 1 and 2 2 , a contradiction to φ \varphi being an acyclic dicolouring. ◊ \lozenge Clearly, one can check in polynomial time whether T T is light. If it is not, then by Claim 0.1 we have χ → a ​ ( T ) ⩾ 3 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\geqslant 3 and we can stop here. From now on, we thus assume that T T is light. An ordered pair ( s , t ) (s,t) of distinct vertices is nice if there exists an acyclic 2 2 -dicolouring φ \varphi of T T such that φ ⁡ ( s ) = φ ⁡ ( t ) = 1 \varphi(s)=\varphi(t)=1 and, for every u ∈ N + ​ ( s ) ∪ N − ​ ( t ) u\in N^{+}(s)\cup N^{-}(t) , φ ⁡ ( u ) = 2 \varphi(u)=2 . Such a colouring φ \varphi is called an ( s , t ) (s,t) -colouring of T T . Claim 0.2 . χ → a ​ ( T ) ⩽ 2 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant 2 if and only if T T admits a nice pair ( s , t ) (s,t) . Proof of claim . By definition, if T T admits a nice pair then it admits an acyclic 2 2 -dicolouring, and in particular χ → a ​ ( T ) ⩽ 2 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant 2 . Conversely, assume that χ → a ​ ( T ) ⩽ 2 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant 2 and let φ \varphi be an acyclic 2 2 -dicolouring of T T . Let V 1 V_{1} and V 2 V_{2} be the vertices coloured respectively 1 1 and 2 2 in φ \varphi , and assume without loss of generality that | V 1 | ⩾ 2 |V_{1}|\geqslant 2 . Since T ⁡ [ V 1 ] T[V_{1}] is transitive, let s s be its sink and t t be its source. By definition, φ \varphi is an acyclic 2 2 -dicolouring of T T such that φ ⁡ ( s ) = φ ⁡ ( t ) = 1 \varphi(s)=\varphi(t)=1 . Moreover, N + ​ ( s ) ∪ N − ​ ( t ) ⊆ V 2 N^{+}(s)\cup N^{-}(t)\subseteq V_{2} , hence showing that φ \varphi is an ( s , t ) (s,t) -colouring. ◊ \lozenge From now on, let us fix two arbitrary distinct vertices s , t s,t . In the remaining of the proof, we show that we can decide in polynomial time whether ( s , t ) (s,t) is a nice pair, and computing an ( s , t ) (s,t) -colouring when it is. Since there exist at most n ⁡ ( n − 1 ) n(n-1) candidates ( s , t ) (s,t) for being a nice pair, the result then follows from Claim 0.2 . We first compute a shortest directed ( s , t ) (s,t) -path P = ( v 0 , v 1 , … , v ℓ − 1 , v ℓ ) P=(v_{0},v_{1},\dots,v_{\ell-1},v_{\ell}) , where s = v 0 s=v_{0} and t = v ℓ t=v_{\ell} (recall that T T is strongly connected). We sequentially build a sequence ( X 0 , … , X ℓ ) (X_{0},\dots,X_{\ell}) of disjoint subsets of V ⁡ ( T ) V(T) where: X 0 \displaystyle X_{0} = N + ​ ( v 0 ) ∖ V ⁡ ( P ) \displaystyle=N^{+}(v_{0})\setminus V(P) and ​ X i \displaystyle\text{and~~}X_{i} = N + ​ ( v i ) ∖ ( V ⁡ ( P ) ∪ ⋃ j = 0 i − 1 X j ) ​ for every i ∈ [ 1 , ℓ ] . \displaystyle=N^{+}(v_{i})\setminus\Big(V(P)\cup\bigcup_{j=0}^{i-1}X_{j}\Big)\text{~~for every $i\in[1,\ell]$.} Furthermore, let X ℓ + 1 = N − ​ ( v ℓ ) ∖ ( V ⁡ ( P ) ∪ ⋃ j = 0 ℓ X j ) X_{\ell+1}=N^{-}(v_{\ell})\setminus\Big(V(P)\cup\bigcup_{j=0}^{\ell}X_{j}\Big) . The point is that now some structure is forced, see Figure 3 for an illustration. s = v 0 s=v_{0} v 1 v_{1} v 2 v_{2} v 3 v_{3} v 4 v_{4} v ℓ 