ozenge We now compute, for every i ∈ [ k ] i\in[k] , the set 𝒞 i \mathcal{C}_{i} of i i -coherent colourings of T ⁡ [ Z i ] T[Z_{i}] , which can be done in polynomial time by the second part of Claim 0.5 . By Claim 0.4 , we know that ( s , t ) (s,t) is a nice pair if and only if there exists a colouring φ \varphi of T T such that, for every i ∈ [ 0 , k ] i\in[0,k] , the restriction of φ \varphi to Z i Z_{i} belongs to 𝒞 i \mathcal{C}_{i} . For every i ∈ [ 0 , k ] i\in[0,k] and colouring φ \varphi of T ⁡ [ Z i ] T[Z_{i}] , we say that φ \varphi is ( ⩽ i ) (\leqslant i) -coherent if there exists an extension φ ⋆ \varphi^{\star} of φ \varphi to T ⁡ [ ⋃ j = 0 i Z j ] T[\bigcup_{j=0}^{i}Z_{j}] whose restriction to Z j Z_{j} belongs to 𝒞 j \mathcal{C}_{j} for every j ∈ [ 0 , i ] j\in[0,i] . We further denote by 𝒞 i ⋆ \mathcal{C}_{i}^{\star} the set of ( ⩽ i ) (\leqslant i) -coherent colourings of T ⁡ [ Z i ] T[Z_{i}] . To decide whether ( s , t ) (s,t) is a nice pair, it only remains to decide whether 𝒞 k ⋆ ≠ ∅ \mathcal{C}_{k}^{\star}\neq\varnothing . Observe that, by definition, 𝒞 0 ⋆ = 𝒞 0 \mathcal{C}_{0}^{\star}=\mathcal{C}_{0} . We can recursively build 𝒞 i ⋆ \mathcal{C}_{i}^{\star} from 𝒞 i − 1 ⋆ \mathcal{C}_{i-1}^{\star} as follows. Claim 0.6 . For every i ∈ [ 1 , k ] i\in[1,k] , 𝒞 i ⋆ = { φ ∈ 𝒞 i ∣ there exists ​ ψ ∈ 𝒞 i − 1 ⋆ ​ such that ​ φ ​ ( u ) = ψ ⁡ ( u ) ​ for all ​ u ∈ Z i ∩ Z i − 1 } . \mathcal{C}_{i}^{\star}=\{\varphi\in\mathcal{C}_{i}\mid\mbox{there exists }\psi\in\mathcal{C}_{i-1}^{\star}\mbox{ such that }\varphi(u)=\psi(u)\text{ for all }u\in Z_{i}\cap Z_{i-1}\}. Proof of claim . Let first φ \varphi be any colouring in 𝒞 i ⋆ \mathcal{C}_{i}^{\star} , and let φ ⋆ \varphi^{\star} be its extension to T ⁡ [ ⋃ j = 0 i Z j ] T[\bigcup_{j=0}^{i}Z_{j}] . Since φ \varphi is exactly the restriction of φ ⋆ \varphi^{\star} to Z i Z_{i} , then φ ∈ 𝒞 i \varphi\in\mathcal{C}_{i} . Moreover, the restriction φ i − 1 \varphi_{i-1} of φ ⋆ \varphi^{\star} to Z i − 1 Z_{i-1} is ( ⩽ i − 1 ) (\leqslant i-1) -coherent, as witnessed by the restriction of φ ⋆ \varphi^{\star} to ⋃ j = 0 i − 1 Z j \bigcup_{j=0}^{i-1}Z_{j} . This shows 𝒞 i ⋆ ⊆ { φ ∈ 𝒞 i ∣ there exists ​ ψ ∈ 𝒞 i − 1 ⋆ ​ such that ​ φ ​ ( u ) = ψ ⁡ ( u ) ​ for all ​ u ∈ Z i ∩ Z i − 1 } . \mathcal{C}_{i}^{\star}\subseteq\{\varphi\in\mathcal{C}_{i}\mid\mbox{there exists }\psi\in\mathcal{C}_{i-1}^{\star}\mbox{ such that }\varphi(u)=\psi(u)\text{ for all }u\in Z_{i}\cap Z_{i-1}\}. For the opposite direction, let φ \varphi be any i i -coherent colouring of T ⁡ [ Z i ] T[Z_{i}] such that there exists ψ ∈ 𝒞 i − 1 ⋆ \psi\in\mathcal{C}_{i-1}^{\star} agreeing with φ \varphi on Z i ∩ Z i − 1 Z_{i}\cap Z_{i-1} . Since ψ \psi is ( ⩽ i − 1 ) (\leqslant i-1) -coherent, there exists an extension ψ ⋆ \psi^{\star} of ψ \psi to ⋃ j = 0 i − 1 Z j \bigcup_{j=0}^{i-1}Z_{j} whose restriction to any set Z j Z_{j} , j ⩽ i − 1 j\leqslant i-1 , is j j -coherent. Let φ ⋆ \varphi^{\star} be the colouring of T [ ⋃ j = 0 i ] T[\bigcup_{j=0}^{i}] equal to φ \varphi on Z i Z_{i} and to ψ ⋆ \psi^{\star} on ⋃ j = 0 i − 1 Z j \bigcup_{j=0}^{i-1}Z_{j} . This is possible because ψ ⋆ ​ ( u ) = ψ ⁡ ( u ) = φ ⁡ ( u ) \psi^{\star}(u)=\psi(u)=\varphi(u) for every u ∈ Z i ∩ Z i − 1 u\in Z_{i}\cap Z_{i-1} , and because Z i ∩ ( ⋃ j = 0 i − 1 Z j ) = Z i ∩ Z i − 1 . Z_{i}\cap\Big(\bigcup_{j=0}^{i-1}Z_{j}\Big)=Z_{i}\cap Z_{i-1}. (1) To see that ( 1 ) holds, we use the definition of the sets Z j Z_{j} s and the fact that V ⁡ ( P ) , X 0 , … , X ℓ + 1 V(P),X_{0},\dots,X_{\ell+1} are pairwise disjoint (by Claim 0.3 (iv) ). Using these facts, we conclude that: Z i ∩ Z i − 1 \displaystyle Z_{i}\cap Z_{i-1} ⊆ Z i ∩ ( ⋃ j = 0 i − 1 Z j ) \displaystyle\subseteq Z_{i}\cap\Big(\bigcup_{j=0}^{i-1}Z_{j}\Big) = ( { v i − 1 , … , v i + 6 } ∪ ⋃ j = i i + 6 X j ) ∩ ( { v 0 , … , v i + 5 } ∪ ⋃ j = 0 i + 5 X j ) \displaystyle=\Big(\{v_{i-1},\dots,v_{i+6}\}\cup\bigcup_{j=i}^{i+6}X_{j}\Big)\cap\Big(\{v_{0},\dots,v_{i+5}\}\cup\bigcup_{j=0}^{i+5}X_{j}\Big) = { v i − 1 , … , v i + 5 } ∪ ⋃ j = i i + 5 X j \displaystyle=\{v_{i-1},\dots,v_{i+5}\}\cup\bigcup_{j=i}^{i+5}X_{j} ⊆ Z i ∩ Z i − 1 , \displaystyle\subseteq Z_{i}\cap Z_{i-1}, implying ( 1 ). It follows by construction that the restriction of φ ⋆ \varphi^{\star} to any set Z j Z_{j} , j ⩽ i j\leqslant i , is j j -coherent, and thus φ ∈ 𝒞 i ⋆ \varphi\in\mathcal{C}_{i}^{\star} . The claim follows. ◊ \lozenge It follows from Claim 0.6 that, given 𝒞 i − 1 ⋆ \mathcal{C}_{i-1}^{\star} , we can build 𝒞 i \mathcal{C}_{i} in polynomial time (since both 𝒞 i \mathcal{C}_{i} and 𝒞 i − 1 ⋆ \mathcal{C}_{i-1}^{\star} have size at most O ⁡ ( n 6 ) O(n^{6}) by Claim 0.5 ). After k ⩽ n k\leqslant n steps, in particular, we know whether 𝒞 k ⋆ \mathcal{C}_{k}^{\star} is empty, and thus whether ( s , t ) (s,t) is a nice pair. Moreover, if ( s , t ) (s,t) is a nice pair, an ( s , t ) (s,t) -colouring φ \varphi can be obtained with a simple backtracking, starting from any colouring of T ⁡ [ Z k ] T[Z_{k}] in 𝒞 k ⋆ \mathcal{C}_{k}^{\star} and, for i = k − 1 i=k-1 to 0 0 , extending it to Z i Z_{i} by choosing any colouring in 𝒞 i ⋆ \mathcal{C}_{i}^{\star} equal to φ \varphi on Z i + 1 Z_{i+1} . ∎ 5 Tight bounds on the acyclic dichromatic number of tournaments Recall that, by Theorem 13 , every tournament of order n n contains a transitive subtournament of order ⌈ log ⁡ n ⌉ \lceil\log n\rceil . It follows that every tournament of order n n admits an acyclic ( n − ⌈ log ⁡ n ⌉ + 1 ) (n-\lceil\log n\rceil+1) -dicolouring, which consists of colouring a largest transitive subtournament with one colour, and using one dedicated colour for every other vertex. We slightly improve on this trivial upper bound by showing the following. Theorem 23 . Let T T be any tournament of order n n , then χ → a ​ ( T ) ⩽ n − α ⋅ log ⁡ ( n ) + 27 , \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant n-\alpha\cdot\log(n)+27, where α = log − 1 ⁡ ( 4 / 3 ) > 2.4 \alpha=\log^{-1}(4/3)>2.4 . It turns out that, up to the multiplicative constant α \alpha , Theorem 23 is best possible. Theorem 24 . There exists n 0 ∈ ℕ n_{0}\in\mathbb{N} such that, for every integer n ⩾ n 0 n\geqslant n_{0} , there exists a tournament T T of order n n with χ → a ​ ( T ) ⩾ n − β ⋅ log ⁡ ( n ) − log ⁡ log ⁡ ( n ) , \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\geqslant n-\beta\cdot\log(n)-\log\log(n), where β = 8 log ⁡ ( 8 / 7 ) < 60 \beta=\frac{8}{\log(8/7)}<60 . Theorems 24 and 23 together raise the following problem. Problem 25 . Find the infimum γ \gamma of all real numbers c c such that there exist infinitely many tournaments T T with χ → a ​ ( T ) ⩾ n − c ⋅ log ⁡ ( n ) . \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\geqslant n-c\cdot\log(n). So far, we have 2.4 < γ < 60 2.4<\gamma<60 . The remaining of this section is devoted to the proofs of Theorems 23 and 24 . Our proof of Theorem 23 relies on the notion of acyclic matchings . A matching ℳ \mathcal{M} in a tournament T T is a set of pairwise disjoint arcs of T T . It is acyclic if, for every pair of arcs u ​ v , x ​ y ∈ ℳ uv,xy\in\mathcal{M} , the oriented bipartite graph T ⁡ [ { u , v , x , y } ] ∖ { u ​ v , x ​ y } T[\{u,v,x,y\}]\setminus\{uv,xy\} is acyclic. Proof of Theorem 23 . Let f : x ↦ α ⋅ log ⁡ ( x ) − 27 ​ ( 1 − 1 x ) f\colon x\mapsto\alpha\cdot\log(x)-27(1-\frac{1}{x}) be defined over ℝ + \mathbb{R}^{+} . We prove by induction on n ∈ ℕ ∗ n\in\mathbb{N}^{\ast} that every tournament T T on n n vertices admits an acyclic matching ℳ \mathcal{M} of size at least f ⁡ ( n ) f(n) . The statement then follows, as an acyclic ( n − | ℳ | ) (n-|\mathcal{M}|) -dicolouring of T T can be obtained by giving a common colour to any pair of vertices matched in ℳ \mathcal{M} , and a private colour to each of the remaining vertices. Observe that f ⁡ ( n ) ⩽ 0 f(n)\leqslant 0 when n ⩽ 11 n\leqslant 11 , so the result trivially holds in this case. We thus assume that n ⩾ 12 n\geqslant 12 . Let u ​ v ∈ A ⁡ ( T ) uv\in A(T) be an arc such that either u u is a source or | N + ​ ( u ) ∩ N − ​ ( v ) | ⩽ n − 3 4 . |N^{+}(u)\cap N^{-}(v)|\leqslant\frac{n-3}{4}. To see that such an arc exists, let v v be any vertex of T T with minimum in-degree, and assume that d − ​ ( v ) ⩾ 1 d^{-}(v)\geqslant 1 (otherwise v ​ w vw may play the role of u ​ v uv for any w ∈ N + ​ ( v ) w\in N^{+}(v) ). Since ∑ v ∈ V ⁡ ( T ) d − ​ ( v ) = ( n 2 ) \sum_{v\in V(T)}d^{-}(v)=\binom{n}{2} , d − ​ ( v ) ⩽ n − 1 2 d^{-}(v)\leqslant\frac{n-1}{2} . Let u ∈ N − ​ ( v ) u\in N^{-}(v) be any vertex whose out-degree is minimum in T ​ [ N − ​ ( v ) ] T[N^{-}(v)] . Again, u u has out-degree at most 1 2 ​ ( n − 1 2 − 1 ) \frac{1}{2}(\frac{n-1}{2}-1) in T ​ [ N − ​ ( v ) ] T[N^{-}(v)] , and it follows that, in T T , | N + ​ ( u ) ∩ N − ​ ( v ) | ⩽ n − 3 4 . |N^{+}(u)\cap N^{-}(v)|\leqslant\frac{n-3}{4}. If u u is a source, let X = ∅ X=\varnothing , and let X = N + ​ ( u ) ∩ N − ​ ( v ) X=N^{+}(u)\cap N^{-}(v) otherwise. Let T ′ T^{\prime} be the tournament T − ( X ∪ { u , v } ) T-(X\cup\{u,v\}) . Note that | V ⁡ ( T ′ ) | ⩾ ⌈ 1 4 ​ ( 3 ​ n − 5 ) ⌉ ⩾ 8 |V(T^{\prime})|\geqslant\lceil\frac{1}{4}(3n-5)\rceil\geqslant 8 , and that f f is increasing over [ 8 , + ∞ ) [8,+\infty) . Therefore, by induction, T ′ T^{\prime} admits an acyclic matching ℳ ′ \mathcal{M}^{\prime} of size at least f ⁡ ( 3 ​ n − 5 4 ) f\left(\frac{3n-5}{4}\right) . Let ℳ = ℳ ′ ∪ { u ​ v } \mathcal{M}=\mathcal{M}^{\prime}\cup\{uv\} . We claim that ℳ \mathcal{M} is acyclic. To see this, let x ​ y xy be any arc of ℳ ′ \mathcal{M}^{\prime} , we only have to show that C = T ⁡ [ { u , v , x , y } ] ∖ { x ​ y , u ​ v } C=T[\{u,v,x,y\}]\setminus\{xy,uv\} is acyclic. Note that C C is an orientation of C 4 C_{4} , so it is acyclic if and only if it contains a source or a sink. Therefore, if x ∈ N + ​ ( u ) ∩ N + ​ ( v ) x\in N^{+}(u)\cap N^{+}(v) or x ∈ N − ​ ( u ) ∩ N − ​ ( v ) x\in N^{-}(u)\cap N^{-}(v) , then C C is acyclic. By construction, of T ′ T^{\prime} , we thus have x ∈ N − ​ ( u ) ∩ N + ​ ( v ) x\in N^{-}(u)\cap N^{+}(v) and analogously y ∈ N −