roperty that, for every k ∈ ℕ k\in\mathbb{N} , the number of proper k k -colourings of G G is exactly 𝒫 ⁡ ( G , k ) \mathscr{P}(G,k) , see for instance [ 12 , Section 14.7] . Stanley’s theorem [ 28 ] draws a connection between the chromatic polynomial of a graph and the number of its acyclic orientations. Theorem 26 (Stanley [ 28 ] ) . Every graph G G admits | 𝒫 ⁡ ( G , − 1 ) | |\mathscr{P}(G,-1)| distinct acyclic orientations. We derive the following. Lemma 27 . There exist 14 acyclic orientations of K 2 , 2 K_{2,2} , 46 acyclic orientations of K 2 , 3 K_{2,3} , and 230 acyclic orientations of K 3 , 3 K_{3,3} . In particular, if D 2 , 2 D_{2,2} , D 2 , 3 D_{2,3} , and D 3 , 3 D_{3,3} are random orientations of K 2 , 2 K_{2,2} , K 2 , 3 K_{2,3} , and K 3 , 3 K_{3,3} respectively, then ℙ ⁡ ( D 2 , 2 ​ is acyclic ) = 7 8 ​ , ​ ℙ ​ ( D 2 , 3 ​ is acyclic ) = 23 32 ​ , and ​ ℙ ​ ( D 3 , 3 ​ is acyclic ) = 115 256 . \mathbb{P}(D_{2,2}\mbox{\rm~is acyclic})=\frac{7}{8}\mbox{,~~~}\mathbb{P}(D_{2,3}\mbox{\rm~is acyclic})=\frac{23}{32}\mbox{,~~~and~~~}\mathbb{P}(D_{3,3}\mbox{\rm~is acyclic})=\frac{115}{256}. Proof. Straightforward counting arguments imply: 𝒫 ⁡ ( K 2 , 2 , x ) \displaystyle\mathscr{P}(K_{2,2},x) = x 4 − 4 ​ x 3 + 6 ​ x 2 − 3 ​ x , \displaystyle=x^{4}-4x^{3}+6x^{2}-3x, 𝒫 ⁡ ( K 2 , 3 , x ) \displaystyle\mathscr{P}(K_{2,3},x) = x 5 − 6 ​ x 4 + 15 ​ x 3 − 17 ​ x 2 + 7 ​ x , \displaystyle=x^{5}-6x^{4}+15x^{3}-17x^{2}+7x, and ​ 𝒫 ​ ( K 3 , 3 , x ) \displaystyle\text{and~~}\mathscr{P}(K_{3,3},x) = x 6 − 9 ​ x 5 + 36 ​ x 4 − 75 ​ x 3 + 78 ​ x 2 − 31 ​ x . \displaystyle=x^{6}-9x^{5}+36x^{4}-75x^{3}+78x^{2}-31x. By Theorem 26 , for integers n , m n,m , the number 𝒜 n , m \mathscr{A}_{n,m} of acyclic orientations of K n , m K_{n,m} is precisely | 𝒫 ⁡ ( K n , m , − 1 ) | |\mathscr{P}(K_{n,m},-1)| . It follows that 𝒜 2 , 2 ​ ( x ) = 14 , ​ 𝒜 2 , 3 ​ ( x ) = 46 , and ​ 𝒜 3 , 3 ​ ( x ) = 230 , \mathscr{A}_{2,2}(x)=14,\text{~~}\mathscr{A}_{2,3}(x)=46,\text{~~and~~}\mathscr{A}_{3,3}(x)=230, as desired. The second part of the statement follows from the observation that K n , m K_{n,m} admits 2 n ​ m 2^{nm} distinct orientations. ∎ We slightly extend the notion of acyclic matching as follows. An acyclic partition in a tournament T T is a collection ( X 1 , … , X t ) (X_{1},\dots,X_{t}) of subsets of V ⁡ ( T ) V(T) such that: ( i ) (i) for each i ∈ [ t ] i\in[t] , 2 ⩽ | X i | ⩽ 3 2\leqslant|X_{i}|\leqslant 3 ; ( i ​ i ) (ii) the sets X 1 , … , X t X_{1},\dots,X_{t} are pairwise disjoint; and ( i ​ i ​ i ) (iii) for all 1 ⩽ i < j ⩽ t 1\leqslant i<j\leqslant t , the oriented bipartite graph T ⁡ [ X i , X j ] T[X_{i},X_{j}] is acyclic. We say that a subset X ⊆ V ⁡ ( T ) X\subseteq V(T) of vertices is partitionable if there exists an acyclic partition ( X 1 , … , X t ) (X_{1},\dots,X_{t}) such that X = ⋃ i = 1 t X i X=\bigcup_{i=1}^{t}X_{i} . We now prove Theorem 24 , which we recall here for convenience. See 24 Proof. We do not give the exact value of n 0 n_{0} , we simply assume that it is large enough so that all upcoming inequalities hold. Let T T be a random tournament on n n vertices (that is, each edge { u , v } \{u,v\} is oriented independently and uniformly at random). Let k = β ⋅ log ⁡ ( n ) + log ⁡ log ⁡ ( n ) k=\beta\cdot\log(n)+\log\log(n) . We first show that, with positive probability, none of the k k -sets X ⊆ V ⁡ ( T ) X\subseteq V(T) is partitionable. We then conclude that, with positive probability, χ → a ​ ( D ) ⩾ n − k \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\geqslant n-k , hence implying the result. Let us fix a pairwise disjoint collection ( X 1 , … , X t ) (X_{1},\dots,X_{t}) of sets of size either 2 2 or 3 3 , and whose union has size ℓ = ⌈ k ⌉ \ell=\lceil k\rceil . We first bound the probability that ( X 1 , … , X t ) (X_{1},\dots,X_{t}) is an acyclic partition in T T . For any 1 ⩽ i < j ⩽ t 1\leqslant i<j\leqslant t , let E i , j E_{i,j} denote the event that the bipartite oriented graph T ⁡ [ X i , X j ] T[X_{i},X_{j}] is acyclic. By 