{i})_{i\in[\zeta]} is an acyclic partition, hence showing that X X is partitionable, a contradiction as | X | ∈ { ℓ , ℓ + 1 } |X|\in\{\ell,\ell+1\} . To see this, let 1 ⩽ i < j ⩽ ζ 1\leqslant i<j\leqslant\zeta . By construction, there exist 1 ⩽ i ′ , j ′ ⩽ h 1\leqslant i^{\prime},j^{\prime}\leqslant h such that Z i ⊆ Y i ′ Z_{i}\subseteq Y_{i^{\prime}} and Z j ⊆ Z j ′ Z_{j}\subseteq Z_{j^{\prime}} . If i ′ ≠ j ′ i^{\prime}\neq j^{\prime} then T ⁡ [ Z i , Z j ] T[Z_{i},Z_{j}] is a subdigraph of T ⁡ [ Y i ′ , Y j ′ ] T[Y_{i^{\prime}},Y_{j^{\prime}}] , and it is acyclic because φ \varphi is an acyclic dicolouring. If i ′ = j ′ i^{\prime}=j^{\prime} , then T ⁡ [ Z i , Z j ] T[Z_{i},Z_{j}] is a subdigraph of T ⁡ [ Y i ′ ] T[Y_{i^{\prime}}] , and it is acyclic because φ \varphi is an acyclic dicolouring. The result follows. ∎ 6 Degenerate graphs Let d d be an integer. A graph G G is d d -degenerate if every non-empty subgraph H H of G G contains a vertex of degree at most d d . It is well-known that 2 2 -degenerate graphs have unbounded acyclic chromatic number (see for instance [ 29 , Corollary 3] ), while 1 1 -degenerate graphs ( i.e. forests) trivially admit an acyclic 2 2 -colouring. In this section, we show a similar behaviour in the directed setting. A digraph is d d -degenerate if its underlying graph is. Given a digraph D D and a partition ( X 1 , X 2 ) (X_{1},X_{2}) of V ⁡ ( D ) V(D) , we denote by D r ​ e ​ v ​ ( X 1 , X 2 ) D^{rev}(X_{1},X_{2}) the digraph D D after reversing the direction of all arcs between X 1 X_{1} and X 2 X_{2} . We will show the following theorem. Theorem 28 . For every 2 2 -degenerate oriented graph D D , there exists a partition ( X 1 , X 2 ) (X_{1},X_{2}) of V ⁡ ( D ) V(D) such that D r ​ e ​ v ​ ( X 1 , X 2 ) D^{rev}(X_{1},X_{2}) is acyclic. Proof. We will prove the theorem by induction on the order, n n , of D D . Clearly the theorem holds when the order is at most 3 3 , since in this case we can use the partition ( ∅ , V ⁡ ( D ) ) (