 X 1 , X 2 ) D^{rev}(X_{1},X_{2}) is acyclic. However, this implies that D ⁡ [ X 1 ] D[X_{1}] and D ⁡ [ X 2 ] D[X_{2}] and D ⁡ [ X 1 , X 2 ] D[X_{1},X_{2}] must all be acyclic as well. So ( X 1 , X 2 ) (X_{1},X_{2}) is the desired colouring. ∎ In particular, we derive the following, as outerplanar graphs are 2 2 -degenerate (see for instance [ 12 , Exercise 10.2.11] ). Corollary 30 . Every oriented outerplanar graph D D satisfies χ → a ​ ( D ) ⩽ 2 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant 2 . We now show that Corollary 29 is best possible by constructing 3 3 -degenerate oriented graphs with arbitrarily large acyclic dichromatic number. We make use of the celebrated result of Ramsey in the following general form, which can be found in many basic textbooks on graph theory, see for instance [ 15 , Theorem 9.1.3] . Theorem 31 ( Ramsey [ 27 ] ) . For all k , b , r ⩾ 1 k,b,r\geqslant 1 , there exists an integer n ⩾ r n\geqslant r such that, for every mapping φ : ( [ n ] b ) → [ k ] \varphi\colon\binom{[n]}{b}\to[k] , there exists X ∈ ( [ n ] r ) X\in\binom{[n]}{r} such that φ ⁡ ( x ) = φ ⁡ ( y ) \varphi(x)=\varphi(y) for all x , y ∈ ( X b ) x,y\in\binom{X}{b} . For integers k , b , r ⩾ 1 k,b,r\geqslant 1 , we denote by R k ​ ( b , r ) R_{k}(b,r) the smallest integer n n for which Theorem 31 holds. Theorem 32 . For every k ∈ ℕ k\in\mathbb{N} , there exists a 3 3 -degenerate oriented graph D D with χ → a ​ ( D ) ⩾ k \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\geqslant k . Proof. Let us fix an arbitrary integer k ∈ ℕ ∗ k\in\mathbb{N}^{\ast} and build a 3 3 -degenerate oriented graph D D with χ → a ​ ( D ) ⩾ k \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\geqslant k . We let D D be the oriented graph obtained as follows. Start with an independent set S S of size ( k − 1 ) ⋅ R k − 1 ​ ( 3 , 4 ) (k-1)\cdot R_{k-1}(3,4) , and take an arbitrary acyclic order ≺ \prec on S S . For every triplet { u , v , w } ⊆ S \{u,v,w\}\subseteq S with u ≺ v ≺ w u\prec v\prec w , we add a vertex x u , v , w x_{u,v,w} and the arcs x u , v , w ​ u x_{u,v,w}u , x u , v , w ​ w x_{u,v,w}w , and v ​ x u , v , w vx_{u,v,w} . Clearly, D D is 3 3 -degenerate since every vertex x ∈ V ⁡ ( D ) ∖ S x\in V(D)\setminus S has degree 3 3 , and S S is an independent set. Assume for a contradiction that χ → a ​ ( D ) ⩽ k − 1 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant k-1 , and let φ \varphi be an acyclic ( k − 1 ) (k-1) -dicolouring of D D . By the pigeonhole principle, there exists a colour i ∈ [ k − 1 ] i\in[k-1] such that S ′ = | S ∩ φ − 1 ​ ( i ) | ⩾ R k − 1 ​ ( 3 , 4 ) S^{\prime}=|S\cap\varphi^{-1}(i)|\geqslant R_{k-1}(3,4) . We define ψ : ( S ′ 3 ) → [ k − 1 ] \psi\colon\binom{S^{\prime}}{3}\to[k-1] as follows: for every triplet { u , v , w } ⊆ S ′ \{u,v,w\}\subseteq S^{\prime} with u ≺ v ≺ w u\prec v\prec w , let ψ ⁡ ( { u , v , w } ) = φ ⁡ ( x u , v , w ) \psi(\{u,v,w\})=\varphi(x_{u,v,w}) . By Theorem 31 and by definition of R k − 1 ​ ( 3 , 4 ) R_{k-1}(3,4) , there exists S ′′ = { s 1 , s 2 , s 3 , s 4 } ⊆ S ′ S^{\prime\prime}=\{s_{1},s_{2},s_{3},s_{4}\}\subseteq S^{\prime} such that ψ \psi is constant on ( S ′ 3 ) \binom{S^{\prime}}{3} . Assume without loss of generality that s 1 ≺ s 2 ≺ s 3 ≺ s 4 s_{1}\prec s_{2}\prec s_{3}\prec s_{4} . Then C = ( s 2 , x s 1 , s 2 , s 3 , s 3 , x s 2 , s 3 , s 4 , s 2 ) C=(s_{2},x_{s_{1},s_{2},s_{3}},s_{3},x_{s_{2},s_{3},s_{4}},s_{2}) is a directed 4 4 -cycle of D D such that φ ⁡ ( s 2 ) = φ ⁡ ( s 4 ) \varphi(s_{2})=\varphi(s_{4}) (because s 2 , s 4 ∈ S ′ s_{2},s_{4}\in S^{\prime} ) and φ ⁡ ( x s 1 , s 2 , s 3 ) = φ ⁡ ( x s 2 , s 3 , s 4 ) = ψ ⁡ ( { s 1 , s 2 , s 3 } ) \varphi(x_{s_{1},s_{2},s_{3}})=\varphi(x_{s_{2},s_{3},s_{4}})=\psi(\{s_{1},s_{2},s_{3}\}) . Therefore, C C is either monochromatic or alternating between two colours, a contradiction to the choice of φ \varphi . ∎ 7 Remarks and further open problems We conclude with a branch of further open problems. We start with the case of planar graphs. In 1979, Borodin [ 13 ] obtained the following celebrated result, resolving a conjecture of Grünbaum [ 18 ] . Theorem 33 (Borodin [ 13 ] ) . Every planar graph admits a proper 5 5 -colouring such that the union of any two colour classes induces a forest. The same bound directly follows for the acyclic dichromatic number. Corollary 34 . Every planar oriented graph D D satisfies χ → a ​ ( D ) ⩽ 5 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant 5 . Proposition 35 . There exists a planar oriented graph D D with χ → a ​ ( D ) ⩾ 3 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\geqslant 3 . Proof. Consider the digraph D D obtained from a directed triangle ( u , v , w ) (u,v,w) by further gluing W ⁡ [ u , v ] W[u,v] , W ⁡ [ v , w ] W[v,w] , and W ⁡ [ w , u ] W[w,u] , where W ⁡ [ x , y ] W[x,y] is the planar oriented graph given in Figure 5 . The statement follows from the observation that, in every 2 2 -dicolouring of W ⁡ [ x , y ] W[x,y] , both x x and y y receive the same colour. Assume for a contradiction that φ \varphi is an acyclic 2 2 -dicolouring of W ⁡ [ x , y ] W[x,y] with φ ⁡ ( x ) = 1 \varphi(x)=1 and φ ⁡ ( y ) = 2 \varphi(y)=2 . Then z 1 z_{1} and z 2 z_{2} cannot receive the same colour, for otherwise either { z 1 , x , z 2 } \{z_{1},x,z_{2}\} or { z 1 , y , z 2 } \{z_{1},y,z_{2}\} induces a monochromatic directed cycle. Moreover, we have φ ⁡ ( z 1 ) = 1 \varphi(z_{1})=1 and φ ⁡ ( z 2 ) = 2 \varphi(z_{2})=2 , for otherwise ( x , y , z 2 , z 1 ) (x,y,z_{2},z_{1}) is a directed cycle alternating between colours 1 1 and 2 2 . Hence φ ⁡ ( z 3 ) = 1 \varphi(z_{3})=1 , otherwise ( y , z 2 , z 3 ) (y,z_{2},z_{3}) is monochromatic. By symmetry, we obtain that φ ⁡ ( z 6 ) = 1 \varphi(z_{6})=1 , φ ⁡ ( z 5 ) = 2 \varphi(z_{5})=2 , and φ ⁡ ( z 4 ) = 1 \varphi(z_{4})=1 . It follows that ( x , z 4 , z 3 ) (x,z_{4},z_{3}) is monochromatic, a contradiction. ∎ x x y y z 1 z_{1} z 2 z_{2} z 3 z_{3} z 4 z_{4} z 5 z_{5} z 6 z_{6} Figure 5: The digraph W ⁡ [ x , y ] W[x,y] together with one of its acyclic 2 2 -dicolourings. We do not believe that the bound of Corollary 34 is tight and pose the following. Conjecture 36 . Every oriented planar graph D D satisfies χ → a ​ ( D ) ⩽ 3 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant 3 . We conclude with several complexity problems. The first one was already mentioned in Section 4.2 . Conjecture 37 . For every fixed k ⩾ 3 k\geqslant 3 it is polynomial to decide whether a tournament T T has χ → a ​ ( T ) ⩽ k \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant k We proved in Section 4 that there exists a polynomial-time algorithm deciding whether a tournament has an acyclic 2 2 -dicolouring. We wonder if such an algorithm can be extended to cover larger classes of oriented graph. Problem 38 . What is the complexity, for every fixed k ∈ ℕ k\in\mathbb{N} , of deciding if a digraph D D with α ⁡ ( D ) ⩽ k \alpha(D)\leqslant k has χ → a ​ ( D ) ⩽ 2 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant 2 ? Already the case when α ⁡ ( D ) = 2 \alpha(D)=2 is open, even for digraphs whose vertex set can be covered by two semicomplete digraphs. A digraph D D is a locally tournament digraph if D ​ [ N + ​ [ v ] ] D[N^{+}[v]] and D ​ [ N − ​ [ v ] ] D[N^{-}[v]] are tournaments for every vertex v v of D D . These digraphs, introduced in [ 5 ] , form a rich generalization of tournaments and share many properties with these [ 8 , Chapter 6] . Problem 39 . What is the complexity of deciding χ → a ​ ( D ) ⩽ 2 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant 2 when D D is a locally tournament digraph? Note that the definition of light still works for locally tournament digraphs and the proof of Claim 0.1 carries over to locally tournament digraphs. Another generalization of tournaments is the class of multipartite tournaments which are orientations of complete multipartite graphs. Problem 40 . What is the complexity of deciding whether a multipartite tournament has acyclic dichromatic number at most 2? This is open already for bipartite tournaments, but polynomial for the special class of multipartite tournaments called extended tournaments . These oriented graphs are of the form D = T ⁡ [ I k 1 , I k 2 , … , I k t ] D=T[I_{k_{1}},I_{k_{2}},\ldots{},I_{k_{t}}] where T T is a tournament and D D is obtained from T T by substituting an independent set of vertices I k j I_{k_{j}} for the j j th vertex of T T for j ∈ [ | V ⁡ ( T ) | ] j\in[|V(T)|] . It is easy to check that if D D is an extension of T T then χ → a ​ ( D ) = χ → a ​ ( T ) \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)=\vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T) . Another generalization of tournaments is the class of quasi-transitive digraphs . These are digraphs containing no induced directed path of length 2. They have a very useful recursive structure. Theorem 41 . [ 4 ] Let D D be a quasi-transitive digraph. (a) If D D is not strongly connected, then there exists a transitive oriented graph T T such that D = T ⁡ [ H 1 , H 2 , … , H t ] D=T[H_{1},H_{2},\ldots{},H_{t}] , where each H i H_{i} is a strongly connected quasi-transitive digraph. (b) If D D is strongly connected, then there exists a strong semicomplete digraph S S such that D = S ⁡ [ Q 1 , Q 2 , … , Q s ] D=S[Q_{1},Q_{2},\ldots{},Q_{s}] where each Q i Q_{i} is either a single vertex or a non-strong quasi-transitive digraph. Note that a strong quasi-transitive digraph D = S ⁡ [ Q 1 , Q 2 , … , Q s ] D=S[Q_{1},Q_{2},\ldots{},Q_{s}] contains the semicomplete digraph S S as an induced subdigraph (just take an arbitrary vertex from each Q i Q_{i} ). Thus, χ → a ​ ( D ) ⩽ 2 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant 2 only if we also have χ → a ​ ( S ) ⩽ 2 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(S)\leqslant 2 . The other direction does not always hold, e.g. in the case when each Q i Q_{i} contains a directed cycle. In fact, the following holds. Theorem 42 . A strong quasi-transitive digraph D = S ⁡ [ Q 1 , Q 2 , … , Q s ] D=S[Q_{1},Q_{2},\ldots{},Q_{s}] has χ → a ​ ( D ) = 2 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)=2 if and only if χ → a ​ ( S ) = 2 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(S)=2 and each Q i Q_{i} is acyclic. Proof. Let D = S ⁡ [ Q 1 , Q 2 , … , Q s ] D=S[Q_{1},Q_{2},\ldots{},Q_{s}] be a strong quasi-transitive digraph without 2-cycles. By the remark above, we may assume that χ → a ​ ( S ) = 2 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(S)=2 . Suppose first that some Q i Q_{i} contains a cycle C C and that χ → a ​ ( D ) = 2 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)=2 . Let φ \varphi be an acyclic 2-dicolouring of D D . Then C C contains an arc u ​ v uv such that φ ⁡ ( u ) = 1 \varphi(u)=1 and φ ⁡ ( v ) = 2 \varphi(v)=2 . Since S S is strong and hence vertex pancyclic by Moon’s theorem [ 7 , Theorem 1.5.1] , there are indices j , k j,k distinct from i i such that D D contains the subdigraph C → 3 ​ [ Q i , Q j , Q k ] \vec{C}_{3}[Q_{i},Q_{j},Q_{k}] . If both of Q j , Q k Q_{j},Q_{k} contain a vertex coloured a ∈ { 1 , 2 } a\in\{1,2\} by φ \varphi then D D contains a monochromatic triangle so we may assume that φ \varphi uses one colour a a on all vertices of Q j Q_{j} and the other colour b ≠ a b\neq a on all vertices of Q k Q_{k} . Combining this with the fact that φ \varphi uses both colours of C C we obtain a contradiction to φ \varphi being an acyclic 2-dicolouring of D D . Assume next that each Q i Q_{i} is acyclic. Fix one vertex s i s_{i} in each Q i Q_{i} and recall that these vertices induce a copy of S S in D D . Now let ψ \psi be an acyclic 2-dicolouring of (this copy of) S S . Then we can extend ψ \psi to an acyclic 2-dicolouring of D D by giving all vertices of Q i Q_{i} the colour ψ ⁡ ( s i ) \psi(s_{i}) . ∎ Notice that above we can extend any acyclic 2-dicolouring of S S to all of D D when each Q i Q_{i} is acyclic so we obtain the following corollary of Theorem 21 . Corollary 43 . There is a polynomial-time algorithm for checking whether a quasi-transitive digraph D D has χ → a ​ ( D ) ⩽ 2 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(D)\leqslant 2 . The concept of acyclic dichromatic number only makes sense for oriented graphs but, due to Theorem 21 , the following problem, which in the language of [ 3 ] can be seen as an orientation completion problem, may be of interest. Here we think of a mixed graph M = ( V , E ∪ A ) M=(V,E\cup{}A) as corresponding to the digraph D = ( V , A ∗ ∪ A ) D=(V,A^{*}\cup A) where A ∗ A^{*} is obtained from E E by replacing every edge in E E by a directed 2-cycle. Equivalently, we are looking for a spanning oriented subgraph of a digraph D D that we obtain by deleting precisely one arc from each 2-cycle of D D . Problem 44 . Is there a polynomial-time algorithm for deciding whether a semicomplete digraph D = ( V , A ) D=(V,A) contains a spanning tournament T = ( V , A ′ ) T=(V,A^{\prime}) with χ → a ​ ( T ) ⩽ 2 \vec{\chi}_{\rm a}(T)\leqslant 2 ? It follows from Corollary 16 that it is NP-complete to decide whether an oriented bipartite graph has acyclic dichromatic number 2. Hence, in general, it is NP-complete to decide whether a bipartite digraph contains a spanning oriented subdigraph with acyclic chromatic number at most 2. However, if the oriented part of a bipartite digraph D D is acyclic, or every arc is in a 2-cycle, then it is easy to see that D D always has a spanning oriented subdigraph with acyclic dichromatic number at most 2. This motivates the following problem. Problem 45 . Classify the class of bipartite digraphs for which it is polynomial to decide the existence of a spanning oriented subdigraph with acyclic dichromatic number at most 2. References [1] P. Aboulker, P. Charbit, and R. Naserasr (2021) Extension of Gyárfás-Sumner conjecture to digraphs . The Electronic Journal of Combinatorics 28 ( P2.27 ). External Links: ISSN 1077-8926 , Link , Document Cited by: §1 . [2] S. D. Andres and W. Hochstättler (2015) Perfect digraphs . Journal of Graph Theory 79 ( 1 ), pp. 21–29 . Cited by: §1 . [3] J. Bang-Jensen, J. Huang, and X. Zhu (2018) Completing orientations of partially oriented graphs . Journal of Graph Theory 87 ( 3 ), pp. 285–304 . Cited by: §7 . [4] J. Bang-Jensen and J. Huang (1995) Quasi-transitive digraphs . Journal of Graph Theory 20 ( 2 ), pp. 141–161 . Cited by: Theorem 41 . [5] J. Bang-Jensen (1990) Locally semicomplete digraphs: A generalization of tournaments . Journal of Graph Theory 14 ( 3